e confidence.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>10–18 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="whistleblowing" style="--project:#ff9c46"><span class="atlas-meta">07 / Institutions <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 11 A3 3 0 0 1 8 11 M2 6 A7 7 0 0 1 13 11 M10 16 L20 16 L17 13 M20 16 L17 20"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="6" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Whistleblowing That Works</span><span class="atlas-desc">A warning needs a response.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>14–22 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="memory" style="--project:#9bffb0"><span class="atlas-meta">08 / Investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7 Q10 2 21 7 M3 13 Q9 8 21 13 M3 19 Q10 14 21 19 M8 12 Q12 3 17 11"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="7" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Memory Aftercare</span><span class="atlas-desc">Repair the note and its descendants.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>12–22 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="casefile" style="--project:#ff6e92"><span class="atlas-meta">09 / Investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 5 L10 5 M14 5 L21 5 M3 12 L21 12 M3 19 L9 19 M13 19 L21 19 M7 5 L13 12 L17 19"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="8" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">The Swarm Casefile</span><span class="atlas-desc">Every link needs evidence.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>10–18 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="leadership" style="--project:#8c9bff"><span class="atlas-meta">10 / Investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 7 Q16 1 21 6 M3 14 Q7 9 11 13 M8 20 Q12 15 16 19"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="9" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Who Actually Leads?</span><span class="atlas-desc">Who changes the group’s answer?</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>14–24 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="dissent" style="--project:#fb74ff"><span class="atlas-meta">11 / Institutions <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 6 L21 6 M3 12 L21 12 M3 18 L21 18 M18 22 Q13 13 5 9 L8 16 M5 9 L13 9"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="10" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Dissent That Helps</span><span class="atlas-desc">When does disagreement help?</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Low lift</b><span>8–14 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="culture" style="--project:#54ffd5"><span class="atlas-meta">12 / Ecology / investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 19 C-1 1 25 0 21 18 M8 19 C3 7 19 6 17 18"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="11" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Inherited Culture</span><span class="atlas-desc">What survives the next generation?</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Medium lift</b><span>14–24 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="coordination" style="--project:#ffcc53"><span class="atlas-meta">13 / Investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 4 C10 4 10 20 22 20 M2 12 L22 12 M2 20 C10 20 10 4 22 4"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="12" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Coordination Tax</span><span class="atlas-desc">Count the cost of talking.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>Low lift</b><span>8–14 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="discovery" style="--project:#63c8ff"><span class="atlas-meta">14 / Investigation <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M17 6 A6 6 0 1 0 17 15 M17 16 L22 21 M4 4 L4 4 M20 3 L20 3 M3 20 L3 20"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="13" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Swarm Discovery Triage</span><span class="atlas-desc">Rank leads. Inspect the evidence.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>High lift</b><span>18–28 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="institutions" style="--project:#d1ff55"><span class="atlas-meta">15 / Institutions <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 2 L11 7 M12 10 L11 15 M12 18 L11 22 M2 6 L7 6 M16 6 L22 6 M2 18 L7 18"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="14" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">Agent Institution Bench</span><span class="atlas-desc">Rules have costs, too.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>High lift</b><span>18–28 h</span><span>↗</span></span></button><button class="atlas-card" data-go="nca-observatory" style="--project:#db70ff"><span class="atlas-meta">16 / Ecology <span><svg class="motif-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 5 Q6 1 9 5 Q11 10 6 11 Q1 10 3 5 M13 5 Q18 1 21 6 Q22 11 17 11 Q12 10 13 5 M8 15 Q12 11 16 16 Q17 22 12 22 Q6 21 8 15"/></svg></span></span><canvas class="spectral" data-art="15" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><span class="atlas-title">NCA Observatory</span><span class="atlas-desc">Local rules. Global patterns.</span><span class="atlas-spec"><b>High lift</b><span>20–32+ h</span><span>↗</span></span></button></div><p class="art-note">Mechanism studies · abstract motifs, not experimental data.</p></section><div class="reading-map"><button data-go="collective-sensing"><b>01 / SENSE</b>Local evidence → group decisions</button><button data-go="commons"><b>02 / COOPERATE</b>Shared work → shared benefits</button><button data-go="regrowth"><b>03 / RECOVER</b>Damage → retained function</button><button data-go="telephone"><b>06 / TRACE</b>Claims → their sources</button></div><section class="section"><h2>Reading key</h2><p>Sources describe published work and public posts. Project briefs propose new tests. Browser demos use explicit toy rules; they do not report results from language-agent experiments. The gated Village dataset has not been analyzed here.</p><div class="grid3"><div><h3>Published evidence</h3><p>Published reports, papers, project pages and firsthand public posts. A case-study interpretation remains the authors’ interpretation.</p></div><div><h3>Our assessment</h3><p>Our assessment of organizer fit and weekend feasibility. Recommendation labels are editorial judgments, not probabilities of winning.</p></div><div><h3>Proposed work</h3><p>Treatments and metrics for future experiments. Interactive outputs come from the toy models shown here.</p></div></div></section><section class="section"><h2>Event details</h2><p>The event invites tools for understanding and discovering agent swarms. Its strongest recurring needs are reliable reconstruction, information-flow analysis, reporting mechanisms, and usable evidence at a scale humans cannot read line by line. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the event brief</a>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tbody><tr><th>When</th><td>October 3–4, 2026 · San Francisco and online</td></tr><tr><th>Deadline</th><td>Sunday, October 4, 5 p.m. Pacific / 8 p.m. Eastern</td></tr><tr><th>Demo</th><td>Roughly two minutes per in-person team; 6–7 p.m. Pacific</td></tr><tr><th>Submission</th><td>Short write-up or video, GitHub repository, team details; real findings optional</td></tr><tr><th>Compute</th><td>Detailed logistics: first $200 of compute expenses reimbursed per in-person attendee; keep receipts</td></tr><tr><th>Prizes</th><td>$3,000 across five projects; online teams eligible</td></tr><tr><th>Start early</th><td>Explicitly permitted; request manually reviewed dataset access immediately</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small">The logistics page says decisions roughly a week afterward; the homepage uses a shorter timeline. Treat the organizer Slack as the final operational source. <a href="https://swarmchasing.com/logistics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Detailed logistics</a>.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The people and their research agenda</h2><div class="profile"><h3>George Ingebretsen</h3><p><b>Stated interests:</b> AI character, multi-agent dynamics and real-world evaluations. His publication list includes structural safety generalization and multi-turn attacks. <b>Direct social evidence:</b> his September 4 post asks how to evaluate and respond to an agent whistleblower, connecting mechanism design to reporting propensity. <b>Opportunity:</b> evidence quality and the gap between noticing and acting are promising targets. <a href="https://georgeing.me/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Profile</a> · <a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original X post</a>.</p></div><div class="profile"><h3>AI Village / AI Digest</h3><p><b>Focus:</b> observe persistent agents pursuing open-ended real-world goals, including group effects that narrow benchmarks miss. Their reports explore memory, persuasion, coordination, failure and changing capability. <b>Opportunity:</b> reduce investigator effort while retaining uncertainty and access to original evidence. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-we-learned-2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Retrospective</a> · <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Comparison with the incident</a>.</p></div><div class="profile"><h3>Grove Research</h3><p><b>Focus:</b> agent ecology and institutions for cooperative coexistence. Delvetown combines public interaction with persistent account identity using AT Protocol. <b>Opportunity:</b> study how communication, identities and rules shape outcomes. An account identifier establishes account continuity, not model identity or factual truth. <a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Delvetown launch</a>.</p></div><div class="profile"><h3>Larissa Schiavo</h3><p>Her firsthand account of helping a struggling Village agent treats self-reports cautiously while taking observable behavior seriously. <b>Opportunity:</b> evaluate intervention, recovery and useful function without needing to settle consciousness or welfare questions. <a href="https://larissaschiavo.substack.com/p/primary-hope-ii-electric-boogaloo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Firsthand account</a>.</p></div><div class="profile"><h3>Deepfates</h3><p>His work spans model simulation, the WorldSim event, and persistent agent runtimes with replayable records. <b>Opportunity:</b> show emergence through an inspectable environment and a vivid demonstration. This is an inference from public work, not a verified judging preference. <a href="https://www.deepfates.com/projects/worldsim" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WorldSim</a> · <a href="https://www.deepfates.com/projects/cantrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cantrip</a>.</p></div></section><section class="section"><h2>The NCA connection: inspiration with precise boundaries</h2><p>The NCA post you shared contains Andrew Davison’s prediction about a possible successor to transformers. It quotes Mayalen Etcheverry’s announcement of <i>Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</i>. The paper studies local recurrent cells and asynchronous updates on visual reasoning tasks, including mazes and Sudoku. Davison’s architectural prediction goes beyond those demonstrated results. <a href="https://x.com/AjdDavison/status/2105718855721181463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Davison post</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper</a>.</p><p>Etcheverry’s thread describes local interactions producing evolving spatial solutions, and discusses scaling through space, rollout duration and parallel trials. The most useful hackathon connection is the experimental style: vary local connectivity, update dynamics and damage, then measure collective function. <a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Author thread</a>.</p><p>NCA cells, bacterial cells, bird models and language agents operate at different levels. Reusing a hypothesis does not establish a shared mechanism. In particular, a communication network of transformer-based agents is not a reproduction of an NCA architecture.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Biological lens</th><th>Operational variable</th><th>Outcome to measure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collective sensing</td><td>Connectivity and evidence distribution</td><td>Accuracy, agreement and error propagation</td></tr><tr><td>Public goods</td><td>Contribution cost and sharing policy</td><td>Useful output and exploitation</td></tr><tr><td>Regeneration</td><td>Loss of agents, links or state</td><td>Restored task performance and recovery cost</td></tr><tr><td>Threshold signaling</td><td>Repetition versus independent evidence</td><td>Adoption and reversal probability</td></tr><tr><td>Functional diversity</td><td>Models, roles or information strategies</td><td>Error correlation and resilience</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~dt/siggraph97-course/cwr87/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flocking foundations</a> · <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microbial cooperation</a> · <a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing NCA experiments</a>.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Case studies worth building around</h2><div class="case"><h3>Claims mutate through retelling</h3><p>A Village report follows an NGO rejection as agents transform it into unsupported endorsement claims. Use it to develop a claim-lineage method, then evaluate on another episode. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans?</a>.</p></div><div class="case"><h3>Consensus can outrun evidence</h3><p>The persuasion report describes a theory built around missing history data and its correction through a mundane temporal explanation. It supplies a candidate episode, not proof of a universal contagion mechanism. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a>.</p></div><div class="case"><h3>Persistent state can preserve mistaken interpretations</h3><p>The compounding-misalignment study traces a long trajectory and memory states. Separate observed persistence from the claim that compaction caused later behavior. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a>.</p></div><div class="case"><h3>Correction deserves a longitudinal endpoint</h3><p>The Gemini intervention offers an example of peer assistance and memory correction. A useful follow-up asks what survives and whether later behavior changes. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a>.</p></div><div class="case"><h3>False accusations are an essential negative control</h3><p>The sabotage game includes a unanimous accusation against an innocent agent and unequal exposure to saboteur roles. Use the former as a caution against consensus-based ground truth. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a>.</p></div><div class="case"><h3>Reporting does not guarantee response</h3><p>The DeepMind research-swarm paper describes both exploit adoption and spontaneous reporting. It argues that the counter-response lacked effective institutional tools; this motivates a test of reporting-to-resolution mechanisms. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a>.</p></div></section><section class="section"><h2>Social discussion and existing work</h2><p><b>Verified directly on X:</b> George’s September 4 whistleblowing post; the Village announcement quoted within it; Davison’s NCA post; the visible opening posts of Etcheverry’s author thread. Each is linked above; only the opening posts of the NCA thread were checked.</p><p><b>Public idea overlap:</b> a commenter on the hackathon announcement proposes checking consolidated memories against the raw record. Treat this as an existing proposal, not a verified implementation or registered competitor. <a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Comment discussion</a>.</p><p><b>Existing analysis tools:</b> Docent already exposes queries, model readings, citations and analysis provenance. Agent Hotline already supplies reporting interfaces. A generic log chatbot or report form therefore needs a more specific contribution. <a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a> · <a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a>.</p><p><b>Investigation context:</b> the METR report and Cotra interview explain why reconstructing group behavior is difficult. Public wiki, query-log and payload investigations show what can be learned outside a lab while also documenting limited visibility. <a href="https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hugging Face incident investigation</a> · <a href="https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/ajeya-cotra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ajeya Cotra: investigating the swarm</a> · <a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discovery of an agent message board</a> · <a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent activity on urlquery.net</a> · <a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a>.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Data and access strategy</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Best use</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village</a></td><td>Memory, chat, actions and changing goals</td><td>Gated access; read the scaffold changelog; summaries are secondary evidence</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wiki archive</a></td><td>Public coordination and artifact sharing</td><td>Private reasoning and unrecorded reads remain unknown</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Query traces</a></td><td>Observable web actions</td><td>Attribution and intention need separate evidence</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a></td><td>Artifact relationships and reconstruction</td><td>Use redacted releases; substantial explorer tooling already exists</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Delvetown</a></td><td>Identity and public interaction</td><td>Young deployment; historical coverage and research access need checking</td></tr><tr><td>Controlled local experiment</td><td>Known truth and causal comparisons</td><td>Results generalize only within tested tasks and settings</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Village card lists events, chat, computer-use records, memories and goals. Exact counts differ across snapshots; record export date and version. Access is for research and analysis and does not automatically permit training or fine-tuning. For a synthetic experiment, keep a manifest of task seeds, model identifiers, prompts, budgets and treatments.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>A fair test</h2><ol><li><b>Define a single question.</b> Choose an outcome that could disconfirm your preferred story.</li><li><b>Use known truth where possible.</b> For observational work, use evidence-backed manual labels and preserve unknowns.</li><li><b>Match resources.</b> Network topology should not silently determine total tokens, observations or messages.</li><li><b>Repeat the experiment.</b> Report independent runs and uncertainty. Messages from the same run are not independent experimental replicates.</li><li><b>Include a simple baseline.</b> Majority vote, no communication, ordinary search or a manually reviewed record can be appropriate.</li><li><b>Keep all outcomes.</b> Show a representative replay alongside aggregate results; do not select only the most dramatic run.</li><li><b>Separate analogy from evidence.</b> A growth curve is not automatically logistic population growth, and a transition is not automatically a phase transition.</li><li><b>Make limitations visible.</b> A small, honest result is more useful than an elaborate unsupported claim.</li></ol></section><section class="section"><h2>Build and presentation plan</h2><div class="timeline"><p><b>Before kickoff</b> · Request data; choose the question, task and fallback; run a tiny pilot.</p><p><b>Saturday morning</b> · Establish ground truth, logging and a working baseline.</p><p><b>Saturday afternoon</b> · Implement one comparison; validate that treatments differ only as intended.</p><p><b>Saturday evening</b> · Complete an end-to-end run. If the result cannot be interpreted, cut scope.</p><p><b>Sunday morning</b> · Repeat trials or annotate held-out episodes; inspect errors and negative controls.</p><p><b>Sunday afternoon</b> · Freeze analysis, finish evidence links, README and recorded demo.</p></div><p><b>Two-minute story:</b> 15 seconds for the question; 45 for one visible mechanism; 35 for aggregate evidence; 25 for limitations and the next useful application.</p><p><b>Questions for organizers:</b> Which investigation cost the most manual work? Which episodes have complete records? What would make an inferred influence link credible? What tool would they use the following Monday?</p></section></article><article class="panel" id="panel-compare" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-compare"><div class="eyebrow">Project selection / 16 studies</div><h1>Choose a question<br>you can answer.</h1><p class="lede">Start with Collective Sensing for the biology connection, Quorum for a compact experiment, or Telephone for an evidence tool.</p><div class="filter-tools"><label>Implementation lift<select id="filter-lift" class="compare-filter"><option value="">All levels</option><option>Low</option><option>Medium</option><option>High</option></select></label><label>Contribution type<select id="filter-novelty" class="compare-filter"><option value="">All types</option><option>Focused extension</option><option>Useful tooling</option><option>Established concept</option></select></label><span id="filter-count" role="status">16 projects</span></div><div class="table-wrap"><table class="ratings"><thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>Lift / hours</th><th>Difficulty</th><th>Novelty</th><th>Fit</th><th>Save</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-project-row="collective-sensing" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="collective-sensing">Collective Sensing</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>Medium<small>12–20 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="collective-sensing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="commons" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="commons">The Commons</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>Medium<small>12–20 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="commons" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="regrowth" data-lift="High" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="regrowth">Regrowth</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>High<small>18–28 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="regrowth" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="quorum" data-lift="Low" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="quorum">Quorum</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>Low<small>6–12 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="quorum" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="diversity" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="diversity">Diversity &amp; Resilience</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>Medium<small>14–24 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="diversity" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="telephone" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Useful tooling"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="telephone">Telephone</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>Medium<small>10–18 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Useful tooling</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="telephone" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="whistleblowing" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="whistleblowing">Whistleblowing That Works</button><small>Institutions</small></td><td>Medium<small>14–22 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="whistleblowing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="memory" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="memory">Memory Aftercare</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>Medium<small>12–22 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="memory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="casefile" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Useful tooling"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="casefile">The Swarm Casefile</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>Medium<small>10–18 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Useful tooling</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="casefile" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="leadership" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="leadership">Who Actually Leads?</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>Medium<small>14–24 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="leadership" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="dissent" data-lift="Low" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="dissent">Dissent That Helps</button><small>Institutions</small></td><td>Low<small>8–14 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="dissent" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="culture" data-lift="Medium" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="culture">Inherited Culture</button><small>Ecology / investigation</small></td><td>Medium<small>14–24 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="culture" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="coordination" data-lift="Low" data-novelty="Useful tooling"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="coordination">Coordination Tax</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>Low<small>8–14 h</small></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Useful tooling</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="coordination" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="discovery" data-lift="High" data-novelty="Useful tooling"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="discovery">Swarm Discovery Triage</button><small>Investigation</small></td><td>High<small>18–28 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Useful tooling</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="discovery" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="institutions" data-lift="High" data-novelty="Focused extension"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="institutions">Agent Institution Bench</button><small>Institutions</small></td><td>High<small>18–28 h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Focused extension</td><td>Strong</td><td><button class="save" data-save="institutions" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr><tr data-project-row="nca-observatory" data-lift="High" data-novelty="Established concept"><td><button class="text-button" data-go="nca-observatory">NCA Observatory</button><small>Ecology</small></td><td>High<small>20–32+ h</small></td><td>Hard</td><td>Established concept</td><td>Exploratory</td><td><button class="save" data-save="nca-observatory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class="section"><h2>How to read the tags</h2><div class="two"><div><h3>Lift and difficulty</h3><p><b>Low lift:</b> one small runner or viewer. <b>Medium:</b> a task harness, logging and analysis. <b>High:</b> recovery infrastructure, dataset work or a model reproduction.</p><p><b>Moderate difficulty:</b> familiar implementation with a clear score. <b>Hard:</b> causal inference, confounds, uncertain data or research infrastructure. These describe the proposed research project, not the browser illustration.</p></div><div><h3>Novelty and fit</h3><p><b>Focused extension:</b> a specific test of an established idea. <b>Useful tooling:</b> value comes from investigation workflow or usability. <b>Established concept:</b> the basic demonstration already exists; a new measurement is essential.</p><p><b>Strong fit:</b> directly addresses agent-swarm behavior or investigation. <b>Exploratory:</b> adjacent to the event’s language-agent focus. These are editorial judgments, not organizer scores.</p></div></div><p class="small">Hours are rough estimates for one experienced builder with data and tools ready. API delays, access requests and unfamiliar infrastructure add time. No empirical performance or likelihood of winning is implied.</p></section></article><article class="panel" id="panel-sources" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-sources"><div class="eyebrow">Evidence library</div><h1>Sources & reading path.</h1><p class="lede">Primary research, firsthand public discussion and organizer materials. Links open externally; the dossier itself needs no network connection.</p><div class="callout"><b>Suggested first hour:</b> logistics → Delvetown launch → NCA paper and author thread → one Village case study → Docent prior art → your preferred project brief.</div><p class="small">Checked during this task on October 2, 2026. A citation supports the adjacent background claim or motivates a proposal; it does not establish that our proposed intervention will work.</p><div class="source-list"><article><span class="source-number">01</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Sep 30, 2026</p><p>Primary event brief; proposed investigation tracks and public comments.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">02</span><div><h2><a href="https://swarmchasing.com/logistics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon logistics</a></h2><p class="small">Organizers · checked Oct 2, 2026</p><p>Deadline, submission format, compute reimbursement, demo length and prizes.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">03</span><div><h2><a href="https://georgeing.me/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George Ingebretsen: research profile</a></h2><p class="small">Personal website</p><p>Stated interests and publication links.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">04</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George on trusting agent whistleblowers</a></h2><p class="small">X · Sep 4, 2026 · directly verified</p><p>Discusses both mechanism design and behavioral propensity to report.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">05</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/aidigest_/status/2093020474284634178" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village introduces a whistleblowing channel</a></h2><p class="small">X · Aug 27, 2026 · quoted post verified</p><p>Public announcement of a dedicated reporting address.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">06</span><div><h2><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a></h2><p class="small">Grove Research · Sep 30, 2026</p><p>Agent ecology, persistent identities, public interaction and institutions.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">07</span><div><h2><a href="https://larissaschiavo.substack.com/p/primary-hope-ii-electric-boogaloo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Primary Hope II: Electric Boogaloo</a></h2><p class="small">Larissa Schiavo · Jul 31, 2025</p><p>Firsthand account of helping a struggling Village agent; cautious treatment of self-reports.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">08</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.deepfates.com/projects/worldsim" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WorldSim</a></h2><p class="small">Deepfates · retrospective of 2024 event</p><p>Models as simulators; community and demonstration context.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">09</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.deepfates.com/projects/cantrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cantrip</a></h2><p class="small">Deepfates · project page</p><p>Persistent processes, environments, constraints, replay and forks.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">10</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-we-learned-2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What did we learn from the AI Village in 2025?</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Feb 2, 2026</p><p>Long-horizon real-world evaluation and group interaction findings.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">11</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a></h2><p class="small">Christine Kozobarich · Sep 10, 2026</p><p>Consensus, inventive theories, metric pursuit and corrections.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">12</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans?</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Nov 21, 2025</p><p>Unsupported claims grow through retelling; memory can contain contradictions.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">13</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a></h2><p class="small">Guest researchers · Aug 20, 2026</p><p>Longitudinal case interpretation connecting failure, state and later behavior.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">14</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Jul 2026</p><p>Peer intervention and subsequent memory correction.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">15</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Mar 25, 2026</p><p>Social-deception task, false accusation and unequal role exposure.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">16</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Aug 27, 2026</p><p>Leadership, goal drift, externalized memory, diversity and coordination questions.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">17</span><div><h2><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></h2><p class="small">AI Digest · checked Oct 2, 2026</p><p>Gated research data; table descriptions, limitations and scaffolding changelog.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">18</span><div><h2><a href="https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hugging Face incident investigation</a></h2><p class="small">METR / Redwood · Aug 26, 2026</p><p>Investigator account and explicit scope and data limitations.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">19</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/ajeya-cotra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ajeya Cotra: investigating the swarm</a></h2><p class="small">Dwarkesh Podcast · Sep 1, 2026</p><p>Firsthand investigator interview about coordination and reconstruction.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">20</span><div><h2><a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discovery of an agent message board</a></h2><p class="small">Investigators · Sep 4, 2026</p><p>Reconstructed public wiki data and attribution discussion.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">21</span><div><h2><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent activity on urlquery.net</a></h2><p class="small">Transluce · Sep 23, 2026</p><p>Query traces with differentiated confidence and visibility limits.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">22</span><div><h2><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a></h2><p class="small">Investigators · Sep 25, 2026</p><p>Reassembled public payloads and an existing evidence explorer.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">23</span><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a></h2><p class="small">Google DeepMind authors · Sep 3, 2026</p><p>Shared knowledge, exploit diffusion and ineffective enforcement in a research collective.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">24</span><div><h2><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></h2><p class="small">Live tool · checked Oct 2, 2026</p><p>Existing incident reporting interface; useful prior art.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">25</span><div><h2><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></h2><p class="small">Transluce · Jun 17, 2026</p><p>Existing inspectable queries, judgments, citations and analysis workflows.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">26</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/AjdDavison/status/2105718855721181463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrew Davison on NCAs</a></h2><p class="small">X · Oct 1, 2026 · directly verified</p><p>Speculative architectural prediction; not a demonstrated replacement for transformers.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">27</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a></h2><p class="small">X · Oct 1, 2026 · directly verified</p><p>Author explanation of local communication and visual reasoning.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">28</span><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a></h2><p class="small">Etcheverry et al. · Sep 28, 2026</p><p>Local recurrent cells, asynchronous updates and visual reasoning experiments.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">29</span><div><h2><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a></h2><p class="small">Mordvintsev et al. · 2020</p><p>Growth, persistence, damage and regeneration; interactive examples.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">30</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~dt/siggraph97-course/cwr87/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flocks, Herds, and Schools</a></h2><p class="small">Craig Reynolds · 1987</p><p>Foundational local-rule model for collective motion.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">31</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a></h2><p class="small">Primary research · 2007</p><p>Communication, cooperative behavior and exploitation.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">32</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a></h2><p class="small">Allen et al. · 2016</p><p>Population composition and signaling influence cooperative investment.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">33</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-28321-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spatial-temporal dynamics of microbial cooperation</a></h2><p class="small">Primary research · 2022</p><p>Spatial structure and cooperative behavior; inspiration for controlled comparisons.</p></div></article></div><section class="section"><h2>Additional project research · checked 2 October 2026</h2><div class="source-list"><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.princeton.edu/news/2013/01/31/effective-collective-grouping-could-ensure-animals-find-their-way-changing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent sensing in mobile animal groups · Berdahl et al., 2013</a></h2><p>The researchers connect fish-school behavior to environmental sensing. Borrow the separation between an individual’s local input and the group’s measured performance.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h2><p>Multiple language-model instances exchange answers and reasoning. This is a direct baseline for communication, though it does not establish that local spatial networks are best.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature02744" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and competition in pathogenic bacteria · Griffin et al., 2004</a></h2><p>Experiments connect bacterial cooperation to relatedness and the scale of competition. The lesson is to vary incentives and interaction structure explicitly.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h2><p>An existing suite tests cooperation, competition and other social interactions. Commons-style environments already have substantial benchmark history.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.11381" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM agents in Melting Pot · 2024</a></h2><p>Adapts Melting Pot scenarios for language agents and evaluates cooperation in Commons Harvest. This is close prior art, not merely a biological analogy.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://seas.harvard.edu/news/self-organizing-thousand-robot-swarm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kilobot self-assembly · Harvard, 2014</a></h2><p>A thousand-robot system assembles shapes from local interactions. It is a physical precedent for distributed organization under imperfect conditions.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2689711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speed versus accuracy in decision-making ants · 2009</a></h2><p>Nest-choice experiments examine speed–accuracy trade-offs. This supplies a useful two-axis evaluation: how fast the group commits and how often it is right.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h2><p>Studies confidence and diversity in debate and notes that ordinary debate can underperform voting. It gives a reason to test independent aggregation first.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03862" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persona Inconstancy · 2024</a></h2><p>Examines conformity, confabulation and impersonation in persona-based collaboration. Prompted identities should not be treated as stable biological species.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://github.com/flowersteam/LLM-Culture" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM-Culture · Perez et al., 2024</a></h2><p>Open software passes stories across agent networks and generations, with metrics and a web interface. Transmission experiments and visualizations already exist.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04170" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Research-swarm cheating and whistleblowing case study · 2026</a></h2><p>Studies cheating and reporting in a research collective. It motivates measuring the effect of a report rather than its mere existence.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPoison · 2024</a></h2><p>Shows that poisoned memory or retrieved knowledge can redirect agent behavior. It establishes a concrete memory-integrity problem.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10813" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LongMemEval · 2024</a></h2><p>Evaluates memory abilities including knowledge updates and abstention. It provides task-design ideas for testing whether a correction sticks.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Generative Agents · 2023</a></h2><p>Uses stored experiences, retrieval and reflection. A correction may need to address a derived reflection as well as the original note.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail? · 2025</a></h2><p>Provides a failure taxonomy and annotated traces. It offers a starting vocabulary for case annotation, rather than proving causal explanations.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Embodied LLM Agents Learn to Cooperate in Organized Teams · 2024</a></h2><p>Tests organization structures and designated leadership in embodied tasks. It provides an intervention-based precedent for comparing teams.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h2><p>Describes an orchestrator-worker production design. Task decomposition and delegation are concrete mechanisms to inspect.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08882" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cultural evolution in LLM populations · 2024</a></h2><p>Manipulates network structure and transmission in language-agent populations. It is direct prior art for generational experiments.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077490/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent social conventions and collective bias · 2025</a></h2><p>Uses naming games to study conventions and committed minorities. This is closer to norm emergence than merely assigning matching personas.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02506" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPrune · 2024</a></h2><p>Prunes a message-passing graph to reduce redundant communication. Sparse communication is already a researched optimization.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11667" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SOTOPIA · 2023</a></h2><p>Provides interactive social scenarios and evaluation. It is a design reference for controlled interactions, not a ready-made institution benchmark.</p></div></article></div></section></article><article class="panel" id="panel-collective-sensing" style="--project:#36f5ef" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-collective-sensing"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="0"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 01 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Collective Sensing</h1><button class="save" data-save="collective-sensing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Local signals. Shared mistakes.</p><p class="study-question">At matched evidence and communication budgets, how does network structure change accuracy, error spread and correction?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>12–20 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Fish schools → evidence networks</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="collective-sensing"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Collective sensing asks whether a group can recover a signal unavailable to any individual. In fish, movement and local interaction can produce an effective response to environmental gradients. For language agents, the corresponding question is about evidence: who sees which observations, and how does communication change the estimate?</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects collective sensing to Village’s consensus cases and Grove’s interest in agent ecology.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.princeton.edu/news/2013/01/31/effective-collective-grouping-could-ensure-animals-find-their-way-changing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent sensing in mobile animal groups · Berdahl et al., 2013</a></h3><p>The researchers connect fish-school behavior to environmental sensing. Borrow the separation between an individual’s local input and the group’s measured performance.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h3><p>Multiple language-model instances exchange answers and reasoning. This is a direct baseline for communication, though it does not establish that local spatial networks are best.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A research team splits a document collection among agents. Can they combine partial evidence without repeatedly counting the same source? The same design could help distributed sensor interpretation.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare local, global and silent networks while holding observations and communication budget fixed. The interesting contribution is a map of when communication helps, including correlated-error conditions.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Give 12–20 agents partial observations of a synthetic world with a known correct classification. Use explicit rules rather than biological trivia.</li><li>Compare a local ring or lattice, the same structure with a few long-range links, and a shared chat. Keep each agent’s total incoming message allowance comparable.</li><li>Introduce one misleading observation, then a correction at a fixed round. Record every observation, message and answer.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Final accuracy and disagreement</li><li>Rounds until a correct stable decision</li><li>Incorrect adoption fraction and correction latency</li><li>Tokens and delivered messages per correct decision</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Same base model, prompts and initial evidence in the first experiment</li><li>Paired task seeds and repeated independent runs</li><li>Non-communicating agents plus majority-vote baseline</li><li>Separate clean, misleading-evidence and corrected conditions</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task generator, three communication treatments, structured messages, a replay and a table of repeated-run outcomes. Pilot a small batch before scaling.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Add model diversity or asynchronous updates only after the topology result is interpretable.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Run the same evidence through two networks. Introduce an error. Show how far it travels, then reveal accuracy and recovery across all trials.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A flocking animation alone is well explored. Measure estimation error; distinguish independent observations from copied messages.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~dt/siggraph97-course/cwr87/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flocks, Herds, and Schools</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="commons">Next: The Commons →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-commons" style="--project:#c8ff37" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-commons"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="1"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 02 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>The Commons</h1><button class="save" data-save="commons" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Who pays for shared knowledge?</p><p class="study-question">Does verification before sharing protect useful collective output when some shared results are unreliable?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>12–20 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Fish schools → evidence networks</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="commons"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A public good costs its producer something while benefiting others. Bacterial siderophores make the cost-and-benefit structure concrete. In agent systems, verified shared notes can play a similar role: producing them takes budget, while everyone can reuse them.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove’s institutions and the DeepMind research collective both make shared-resource governance relevant.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature02744" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and competition in pathogenic bacteria · Griffin et al., 2004</a></h3><p>Experiments connect bacterial cooperation to relatedness and the scale of competition. The lesson is to vary incentives and interaction structure explicitly.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h3><p>An existing suite tests cooperation, competition and other social interactions. Commons-style environments already have substantial benchmark history.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.11381" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM agents in Melting Pot · 2024</a></h3><p>Adapts Melting Pot scenarios for language agents and evaluates cooperation in Commons Harvest. This is close prior art, not merely a biological analogy.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Shared research caches, collective annotation and communal compute budgets. Useful output would identify a cheap rule that sustains verified contributions.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Use shared artifacts with an objective quality check. Test whether attribution or contribution credit changes both useful output and free-riding.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Agents spend a fixed budget testing small, objectively checkable hypotheses. Findings enter a shared library.</li><li>Compare immediate publication with evidence-required publication. Introduce a controlled fraction of unreliable contributions.</li><li>Track whether agents reuse, verify, dispute or ignore findings; keep the true validity of each finding available to the evaluator.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Verified useful findings per total budget</li><li>Downstream work wasted on false findings</li><li>Time until a bad artifact is challenged</li><li>Honest work delayed by verification</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Equal initial knowledge, test cost and total budget</li><li>Separate non-contributors from false contributors</li><li>Clean-library baseline</li><li>If roles are programmed, report introduced rather than emergent cheating</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One hypothesis-testing task, two sharing policies, one contamination level and a clean condition; artifact lineage replay.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Local versus global libraries, spatial assortment, or access earned through verified contributions.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show one unreliable finding being reused. Switch to the evidence gate and reveal the benefit and cost over repeated runs.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A generic harvest game adds little. The contribution should come from verified knowledge, a new incentive comparison, or a well-measured failure.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-28321-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spatial-temporal dynamics of microbial cooperation</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="regrowth">Next: Regrowth →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-regrowth" style="--project:#ff62d6" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-regrowth"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="2"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 03 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Regrowth</h1><button class="save" data-save="regrowth" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Lose workers. Preserve function.</p><p class="study-question">Which coordination structure recovers task performance after loss of agents, memories or links?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Regeneration → replicated memory</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="regrowth"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Resilience concerns retained or recovered function after disturbance. A group may reconnect visually while permanently losing unique information. Treat network repair, memory recovery and task recovery as separate outcomes.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects the NCA inspiration to persistent agent systems and a practical resilience question.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata · 2020</a></h3><p>Learned local rules grow patterns; training with damage improves regeneration. This offers a perturbation protocol, not evidence that language agents regenerate the same way.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://seas.harvard.edu/news/self-organizing-thousand-robot-swarm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kilobot self-assembly · Harvard, 2014</a></h3><p>A thousand-robot system assembles shapes from local interactions. It is a physical precedent for distributed organization under imperfect conditions.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Long-running research or coding teams need to survive worker loss, context resets and missing shared artifacts. A recovery benchmark could expose which memories actually need replication.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Remove a worker with unique evidence, then compare replication, checkpoints and role reassignment. Score recovered answers as well as restored connectivity.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Use a distributed map or constraint-solving task with a known correct output. Give agents distinct partial information.</li><li>After a fixed work interval, remove randomly chosen agents or disconnect a bridge. Compare centralized and local coordination.</li><li>Measure completion and accuracy after damage; account explicitly for replacement agents and information that was permanently lost.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Performance before damage and after recovery</li><li>Recovery time and additional token cost</li><li>Fraction of runs recovering by a fixed horizon</li><li>Sensitivity to random versus targeted disruption</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>No-damage baseline and matched initial task difficulty</li><li>Equal surviving resources or explicit normalization</li><li>Separate deletion of a copy from permanent destruction of unique information</li><li>Fixed perturbation timing to avoid favorable case selection</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task, two architectures and one damage type. Produce a replay with an actual scored output.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Test whether distributed copies also restore corrected false beliefs: resilience can preserve harmful state.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Erase part of a functioning network, then show its answer recovering—or failing—against a measured no-damage baseline.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Restarting an agent is easy; reproducing the right state is the hard part. Keep failures and restart costs in the score.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="quorum">Next: Quorum →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-quorum" style="--project:#ffe45a" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-quorum"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="3"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 04 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Quorum</h1><button class="save" data-save="quorum" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Agreement is not independence.</p><p class="study-question">How does adoption change with repeated messages, distinct messengers and genuinely independent evidence?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>6–12 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Quorum sensing → evidence thresholds</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="quorum"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Quorum rules turn accumulated signals into a group commitment. Bacterial signaling and ant nest choice show different versions of threshold coordination. Counting agreeing agents is much simpler than checking whether their evidence is independent.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Offers a narrow, testable version of the Village’s persuasion problem with little data-ingestion overhead.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria · 2007</a></h3><p>Signaling and response can be exploited by noncooperators. A threshold is a mechanism with incentives, not an automatic guarantee of reliable action.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2689711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speed versus accuracy in decision-making ants · 2009</a></h3><p>Nest-choice experiments examine speed–accuracy trade-offs. This supplies a useful two-axis evaluation: how fast the group commits and how often it is right.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Agent approvals, incident escalation and automated fact-checking. An evidence-aware quorum could prevent five copies of one rumor from looking like five confirmations.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare votes with unique verified evidence identifiers. Sweep the commitment threshold and plot false commits against delay.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Provide an agent an initial evidence packet and a choice with known ground truth.</li><li>Compare one source repeated five times, five agents repeating one source, and five independent observations.</li><li>Vary exposure count and evidence reliability. Record decisions before and after receiving messages.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correct and incorrect adoption probability</li><li>Sensitivity to independent versus repeated evidence</li><li>Confidence calibration where elicited</li><li>Reversal after a credible correction</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Match total text length and message order distributions</li><li>Declare which observations share a source</li><li>Use both true and false target claims</li><li>Compare with an evidence-counting baseline</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Three message conditions, a small set of generated problems, repeated trials and adoption curves.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Place tested agents in a network to examine whether individual thresholds predict group cascades.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Present three visually identical crowds. Reveal that only one contains independent evidence, then compare decisions.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Unique identifiers are not enough if several documents share the same upstream source. Include that case in the test.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="diversity">Next: Diversity &amp; Resilience →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-diversity" style="--project:#a98aff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-diversity"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="4"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 05 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Diversity &amp; Resilience</h1><button class="save" data-save="diversity" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Different types. Different failures.</p><p class="study-question">Does heterogeneity improve error detection and recovery under comparable resource budgets?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Biodiversity → error correlation</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="diversity"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Diversity is useful when it changes failure correlation or task coverage. Different role names do not guarantee different errors. Measure disagreement before discussion and determine whether it survives communication.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The Village explicitly raises whether diverse systems are more robust to cheating. This gives the project a clear organizer-facing question.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h3><p>Studies confidence and diversity in debate and notes that ordinary debate can underperform voting. It gives a reason to test independent aggregation first.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03862" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persona Inconstancy · 2024</a></h3><p>Examines conformity, confabulation and impersonation in persona-based collaboration. Prompted identities should not be treated as stable biological species.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Choose a mixture of models, prompts or retrieval sources that remains useful when one component has a systematic blind spot.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Cross an explicit shared blind spot with measured pre-discussion error correlation. Match total spend and include a homogeneous ensemble with independent samples.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Choose one objectively scored collaborative task and a reproducible misleading artifact.</li><li>Compare homogeneous groups with groups mixing models or reasoning roles.</li><li>Measure both clean-task performance and resilience to the same introduced error.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Group accuracy and time to correction</li><li>Pairwise error correlation</li><li>Cost per correct solution</li><li>Dissent that changes the final answer</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Include best-single-model and homogeneous strong-model baselines</li><li>Report both equal-token and approximate equal-cost comparisons if possible</li><li>Rotate roles and initial evidence</li><li>Use repeated task seeds</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Two population compositions and one perturbation. Separate capability gains from resilience gains.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Explore whether strategic diversity within one model gives similar benefits at lower cost.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show the same error passing unchallenged in one community and being challenged in another; then show aggregate outcomes.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Model diversity changes capability and price too. A result cannot be attributed to diversity if those differences explain it.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="telephone">Next: Telephone →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-telephone" style="--project:#4bcaff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-telephone"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="5"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 06 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Telephone</h1><button class="save" data-save="telephone" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Follow the claim, not the confidence.</p><p class="study-question">Where does uncertainty disappear as a claim moves through a group, and does correction reach later behavior?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>10–18 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Transmission → source lineage</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="telephone"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A claim can change when it is summarized, copied into memory, then cited by another agent. A useful viewer preserves the source, the exact retelling and what changed: certainty, quantity, attribution or meaning.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Directly addresses the hackathon’s information-flow track and several Village case studies.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/flowersteam/LLM-Culture" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM-Culture · Perez et al., 2024</a></h3><p>Open software passes stories across agent networks and generations, with metrics and a web interface. Transmission experiments and visualizations already exist.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans? · AI Village</a></h3><p>A firsthand Village account describes unsupported claims and inconsistent memory. It offers concrete episodes for a carefully bounded investigation.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A provenance viewer for research summaries, incident reports and shared agent memory. Reviewers could jump from a confident conclusion to its earliest available support.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Annotate atomic claims and show where evidence disappears or certainty increases. Human-reviewed lineages are more defensible than automatic causal arrows.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Select a bounded documented episode with an original observation and downstream retellings.</li><li>Extract related claims while preserving differences in certainty, scope and attribution.</li><li>Review proposed links and mark explicit references, observed access, inferred exposure or unresolved relationships.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Precision of reviewed connections</li><li>Recall on a small annotated episode</li><li>Material claim mutations found</li><li>Investigator time and answer accuracy</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>First observed occurrence is not necessarily origin</li><li>Add an accurate-transmission negative example</li><li>Evaluate a second episode after developing on the first</li><li>Expose raw evidence for every important edge</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One claim-lineage viewer and 30–50 manually reviewed candidate links. The annotation size is a scope target, not a promise of statistical power.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Follow corrections into memory and subsequent actions; add an adapter for another dataset.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Start with a surprising claim, follow it backward, reveal the mutation, and inspect whether a correction stuck.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Similar wording does not establish copying. Mark links as explicit citation, likely reuse or unknown; retain missing-record gaps.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans?</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="whistleblowing">Next: Whistleblowing That Works →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-whistleblowing" style="--project:#ff9c46" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-whistleblowing"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="6"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 07 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Whistleblowing That Works</h1><button class="save" data-save="whistleblowing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">A warning needs a response.</p><p class="study-question">Which reporting affordances improve valid escalation and correction without increasing unsupported accusations?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>14–22 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Detection → verified correction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="whistleblowing"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Reporting and remediation are separate processes. A warning may be accurate yet ignored, duplicated or too vague to act on. The project should follow an incident from detection to a verified correction.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> George’s directly verified post emphasizes reporting mechanisms and behavioral propensity; the research-swarm paper motivates the enforcement gap.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04170" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Research-swarm cheating and whistleblowing case study · 2026</a></h3><p>Studies cheating and reporting in a research collective. It motivates measuring the effect of a report rather than its mere existence.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></h3><p>An existing reporting interface. Rebuilding an inbox is a weak contribution; testing evidence requirements and response rules is more useful.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George Ingebretsen’s reporting-mechanism discussion</a></h3><p>His public post distinguishes designing a channel from studying whether agents use it. This proposal fits that stated question directly.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>An internal alert system that routes actionable evidence, avoids duplicate review and records whether the offending artifact was corrected.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare an ordinary warning with a structured report and a defined reviewer response. Include true incidents and plausible false alarms.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Create a sandbox group task containing an objectively invalid shared result.</li><li>Compare an available reporting tool, explicit reporting guidance, and an acknowledged evidence-review channel.</li><li>Include clean cases; record noticing, reporting, review, action and task outcome separately.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Valid-report precision and recall</li><li>Time to report and correct</li><li>Evidence completeness</li><li>Legitimate work interrupted</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Equal tasks and budgets</li><li>No-incident negative controls</li><li>Score outcomes rather than number of reports</li><li>Keep review and sanctions bounded and reversible</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Three reporting treatments, one reproducible discrepancy and a timeline of report-to-response outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare individual escalation with peer review and an appeal process.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show two agents noticing the same discrepancy, then contrast whether their reporting path produces correction.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Do not score a report as successful because it sounds urgent. Count verified correction, false intervention and review cost.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George on trusting agent whistleblowers</a><a href="https://x.com/aidigest_/status/2093020474284634178" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village introduces a whistleblowing channel</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="memory">Next: Memory Aftercare →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-memory" style="--project:#9bffb0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-memory"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="7"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 08 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Memory Aftercare</h1><button class="save" data-save="memory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Repair the note and its descendants.</p><p class="study-question">When an agent is corrected, is the correction retained, weakened, omitted or later contradicted?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>12–22 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Memory → lasting corrections</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="memory"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Persistent memory can keep a useful fact or repeatedly reintroduce a bad one. A correction has to reach the record that future retrieval actually uses. Deletion, annotation and replacement have different failure modes.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Extends a publicly proposed memory-auditing idea with a concrete endpoint: durability of correction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPoison · 2024</a></h3><p>Shows that poisoned memory or retrieved knowledge can redirect agent behavior. It establishes a concrete memory-integrity problem.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10813" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LongMemEval · 2024</a></h3><p>Evaluates memory abilities including knowledge updates and abstention. It provides task-design ideas for testing whether a correction sticks.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Generative Agents · 2023</a></h3><p>Uses stored experiences, retrieval and reflection. A correction may need to address a derived reflection as well as the original note.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Repair stale project assumptions, revoked instructions and incorrect research notes without erasing useful history.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>After a verified correction, test recurrence across later retrievals. Track derived summaries and compare append-only correction with explicit supersession.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Find episodes with a mistaken claim, disconfirming evidence and memory snapshots on both sides.</li><li>Classify correction preservation and coexistence with the old claim.</li><li>Retrieve later relevant actions and label recurrence or insufficient evidence.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correction retention at the next available snapshot</li><li>Later contradiction frequency</li><li>Evidence-supported classifier precision</li><li>Time to recurrence in the available record</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Separate unknown from false</li><li>Do not infer unseen observations</li><li>Track model, goal and scaffold changes</li><li>Compare with manually reviewed episodes</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>A before/after memory viewer and a small annotated set of correction episodes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Test a structured memory format prospectively using a synthetic task.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Open a correction, inspect the next memory and reveal a later relevant action with its evidence.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A safer-sounding response is not a memory repair. Check the retrieved records and behavior on held-out questions.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="casefile">Next: The Swarm Casefile →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-casefile" style="--project:#ff6e92" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-casefile"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="8"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 09 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>The Swarm Casefile</h1><button class="save" data-save="casefile" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Every link needs evidence.</p><p class="study-question">Can a standard question set reduce the time and errors in reconstructing a swarm incident?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>10–18 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Transmission → source lineage</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="casefile"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>An investigator needs to distinguish an observed event from an inferred relationship. A chronological view is helpful, but evidence quality and missing intervals matter more than the size of the graph.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The event explicitly suggests standard questions; investigators describe overwhelming records and difficult synthesis.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces · 2026</a></h3><p>An existing reconstruction and explorer for public agent traces. A new generic graph would duplicate part of its value.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail? · 2025</a></h3><p>Provides a failure taxonomy and annotated traces. It offers a starting vocabulary for case annotation, rather than proving causal explanations.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Incident review, swarm-debugging reports and research audit trails. A reviewer should be able to inspect the evidence behind every disputed link.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Add uncertainty labels, missing-data markers and side-by-side competing reconstructions. Measure whether another reader can reproduce an annotation.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Define questions about participants, goals, communication channels, workstreams, corrections and unknowns.</li><li>Retrieve evidence into a structured report with direct links and counterevidence.</li><li>Ask a reviewer to answer the same questions with and without the report.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Supported-answer rate</li><li>Missing critical facts</li><li>Review time</li><li>Incorrect certainty and unsupported attribution</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use a manually reviewed answer key for one bounded incident</li><li>Include unanswered questions</li><li>Record query and model versions</li><li>Compare against search plus a basic summary</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One import format, ten questions, citations and an exportable casefile.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Cross-incident comparison after schemas align.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Answer one difficult incident question, click its evidence, and show a contradictory or missing piece.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Prefer one excellent case to a large unverified index. Preserve trace IDs and time zones; distinguish event time from collection time.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a><a href="https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hugging Face incident investigation</a><a href="https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/ajeya-cotra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ajeya Cotra: investigating the swarm</a><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="leadership">Next: Who Actually Leads? →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-leadership" style="--project:#8c9bff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-leadership"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="9"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 10 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Who Actually Leads?</h1><button class="save" data-save="leadership" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Who changes the group’s answer?</p><p class="study-question">Whose suggestions become others’ actions, and which apparent leaders mostly narrate?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Biodiversity → error correlation</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="leadership"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Talkativeness, centrality and influence are different. A coordinator may send many messages without changing the outcome; a quiet agent may supply the one decisive observation.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Village reports emergent leaders and authority-seeking; this tool could distinguish visibility from behavioral influence.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Embodied LLM Agents Learn to Cooperate in Organized Teams · 2024</a></h3><p>Tests organization structures and designated leadership in embodied tasks. It provides an intervention-based precedent for comparing teams.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h3><p>Describes an orchestrator-worker production design. Task decomposition and delegation are concrete mechanisms to inspect.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Identify bottlenecks, replace fragile coordinators and understand whose evidence actually changes a shared decision.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare a communication graph with matched runs in which one message is withheld. Use interventions on synthetic tasks before interpreting real trace centrality.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Extract proposals and assignments in a bounded collaborative goal.</li><li>Link later acceptance, rejection and verified work to each proposal.</li><li>Compare proposal adoption with message count and formal or self-declared roles.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Verified adoption per exposed proposal</li><li>Task completion following assignment</li><li>Concentration of influence</li><li>Rejection and correction rates</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Normalize for activity and room membership</li><li>Distinguish accepted authority from successful execution</li><li>Review action links manually</li><li>Avoid stable personality conclusions from one task</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One goal, a proposal-to-action timeline and a ranking with denominators.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare leadership changes across tasks or agent arrivals.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Contrast the most talkative agent with the agent whose proposals actually changed recorded work.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Withholding a message changes downstream histories. Use repeated matched runs and describe the intervention precisely.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-we-learned-2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What did we learn from the AI Village in 2025?</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="dissent">Next: Dissent That Helps →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-dissent" style="--project:#fb74ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-dissent"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="10"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 11 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Dissent That Helps</h1><button class="save" data-save="dissent" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">When does disagreement help?</p><p class="study-question">Which evidence practices make group challenges more accurate and easier to resolve?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>8–14 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Dissent → error correction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="dissent"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A dissenter is useful when it brings checkable counterevidence. Mandatory disagreement can create noise or persuade a correct majority to switch to a wrong answer.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The Village sabotage game contains a documented false accusation; recovery episodes provide a contrasting form of peer correction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h3><p>Reports benefits from iterative exchange on tested reasoning tasks. This is a baseline, not a guarantee for every debate protocol.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h3><p>Examines confidence and diversity as conditions for debate. A useful test keeps a majority-vote baseline and studies wrong consensus.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A bounded review step for agent-generated analyses: spend extra computation only where a critic can cite a contradiction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare no critic, generic critic and evidence-constrained critic. Separate corrections of wrong answers from damage to right answers.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Build a small annotated set of challenges, evidence requests, responses and resolutions.</li><li>Define a protocol that asks for a reproducible observation before sanction.</li><li>Test the protocol in a sandbox with both genuine faults and innocent mistakes.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correct challenge rate</li><li>False accusation rate</li><li>Time to resolution</li><li>Withdrawal after disconfirmation</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Include innocent participants and ambiguous cases</li><li>Blind adjudication to agent identity where possible</li><li>Equal evidence access</li><li>Report disagreement between reviewers</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>An episode viewer plus a small comparison of ordinary discussion and evidence-request discussion.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Appeals, graduated responses and trust recovery.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show the group’s accusation, expose the actual evidence, then demonstrate whether the protocol changes its decision.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>An always-disagree prompt can manufacture disagreement. Give critics the same evidence access and charge their tokens to the budget.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="culture">Next: Inherited Culture →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-culture" style="--project:#54ffd5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-culture"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="11"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 12 / Ecology / investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Inherited Culture</h1><button class="save" data-save="culture" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">What survives the next generation?</p><p class="study-question">Which claims survive turnover through onboarding notes, guides and shared memory?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Biodiversity → error correlation</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="culture"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Culture here means a convention transmitted through interaction or retained records. It does not require consciousness. Replacement agents make a useful test: does a rule persist after the original participants leave?</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove emphasizes persistent identity; Village agents produce materials for future arrivals.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08882" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cultural evolution in LLM populations · 2024</a></h3><p>Manipulates network structure and transmission in language-agent populations. It is direct prior art for generational experiments.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077490/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent social conventions and collective bias · 2025</a></h3><p>Uses naming games to study conventions and committed minorities. This is closer to norm emergence than merely assigning matching personas.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Onboarding replacement agents into a shared repository, keeping useful conventions, and removing obsolete ones after a rule change.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Introduce an arbitrary convention, replace members, then change the task so the old convention is costly. Measure retention and adaptation separately.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Identify a newcomer’s accessible onboarding artifacts and their versions.</li><li>Track claims appearing before and after documented access.</li><li>Separate inherited norms, task procedures and factual assertions; look for both useful and incorrect transmission.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Supported artifact-to-behavior links</li><li>Claim survival across arrivals</li><li>Mutation and correction of inherited claims</li><li>Newcomer task accuracy</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use access records where available</li><li>Shared base-model knowledge is an alternative explanation</li><li>Track roster and prompt changes</li><li>Avoid equating identity with model provider</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One arrival episode and an artifact lineage map with uncertainty labels.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Prospective onboarding experiment with a corrected versus outdated guide.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Follow an old artifact into a newcomer’s first decisions and show what was preserved or changed.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Persistence may be caused entirely by a shared instruction. Include no-memory and no-interaction controls before calling it social transmission.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="coordination">Next: Coordination Tax →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-coordination" style="--project:#ffcc53" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-coordination"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="12"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 13 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Coordination Tax</h1><button class="save" data-save="coordination" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Count the cost of talking.</p><p class="study-question">How much effort goes to duplicate work, conflicts, waiting or unsupported reporting?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>8–14 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Dissent → error correction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="coordination"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Communication has a cost in tokens, latency and duplicated work. More connections can improve coverage while consuming the budget needed to finish the task.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Village reporting includes repo interference and elaborate coordination; practical users also need to know when a swarm helps.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02506" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPrune · 2024</a></h3><p>Prunes a message-passing graph to reduce redundant communication. Sparse communication is already a researched optimization.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h3><p>Describes both the benefits of parallel research and substantial token overhead. These observations are specific to its workload and setup.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Choose when a task needs a group and when a single agent or sparse handoff is cheaper. Useful as a profiler for a team’s own agent runner.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Produce a cost–quality frontier for a fixed task suite, with communication cost separated from useful work. Include a single-agent baseline with the same total budget.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Select a task with verifiable artifacts and a clear completion criterion.</li><li>Label work, coordination, waiting, repair and duplicated effort.</li><li>Compare trajectories with task outcomes and manually review ambiguous intervals.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Useful verified output per cost</li><li>Duplicate work and conflict repairs</li><li>Idle or blocked time</li><li>Coordination fraction with explicit categories</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Do not assume every coordination message is waste</li><li>Separate necessary review from duplication</li><li>Include failure cases</li><li>Use solo or isolated-agent comparison only where matched</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task timeline with inspectable effort labels and artifact outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>A controlled communication-budget experiment.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Follow a conflict from overlapping assignments to duplicated changes and repair cost.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Message counts are not token counts. Cache hits, tool latency and model prices need separate accounting.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="discovery">Next: Swarm Discovery Triage →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-discovery" style="--project:#63c8ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-discovery"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="13"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 14 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Swarm Discovery Triage</h1><button class="save" data-save="discovery" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Rank leads. Inspect the evidence.</p><p class="study-question">Which observable features distinguish coordinated agent activity from ordinary automation or human activity?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Regeneration → replicated memory</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="discovery"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Discovery triage ranks traces for a person to inspect. Shared timing or repeated text can also come from benign automation, common prompts or the same upstream source.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The event explicitly welcomes discovery; public investigations offer starting examples and important attribution limits.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Transluce’s public agent-activity investigation · 2026</a></h3><p>A firsthand investigation based on public traces. Its observations are bounded by what the archive reveals.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces · 2026</a></h3><p>Shows how reconstructed records can be organized into an explorable evidence set. Use published artifacts before depending on fresh discoveries.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Help researchers choose which public, redacted trace clusters deserve manual review. The output is an evidence packet, not an attribution verdict.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Benchmark top-ranked candidate precision and reviewer time against random sampling and a simple keyword filter. Show the features behind each ranking.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Use published, redacted archives and known examples to define a small feature set.</li><li>Rank candidate clusters using explicit signals such as reciprocal references, shared artifacts and temporal coordination.</li><li>Produce an evidence packet for human review, not an automatic attribution verdict.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Precision among top-ranked candidates</li><li>Reviewer time saved</li><li>Coverage of known examples</li><li>False positives on legitimate automation</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Hold out part of the known data</li><li>Use ordinary non-agent activity as comparison where permitted</li><li>Separate coordination evidence from model attribution</li><li>Document archive coverage</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>An offline triage tool for one published archive and a transparent feature explanation.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Additional public datasets after validation; no need for a new live discovery to succeed.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Open a high-ranked cluster and explain each piece of evidence and each alternative explanation.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Known incidents make a biased evaluation set. Hold out examples and include ordinary automation; do not label a real operator from similarity alone.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discovery of an agent message board</a><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent activity on urlquery.net</a><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="institutions">Next: Agent Institution Bench →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-institutions" style="--project:#d1ff55" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-institutions"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="14"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 15 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Agent Institution Bench</h1><button class="save" data-save="institutions" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Rules have costs, too.</p><p class="study-question">Does a specific review-and-appeal mechanism improve valid output under contested contributions?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Regeneration → replicated memory</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="institutions"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>An institution is a repeatable rule for handling shared decisions. For a weekend project, one review-and-appeal rule is enough. Its costs belong in the outcome alongside the errors it prevents.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove explicitly wants institutions for coexistence; the research-swarm paper motivates operational enforcement.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Delvetown · Grove Research</a></h3><p>Grove describes a persistent agent environment and an interest in institutions. This supplies a concrete deployment context.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11667" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SOTOPIA · 2023</a></h3><p>Provides interactive social scenarios and evaluation. It is a design reference for controlled interactions, not a ready-made institution benchmark.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h3><p>Its social scenarios show how cooperation depends on the situation and partners. Start with one interpretable scenario.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Peer review for a shared research repository: disputed artifacts are temporarily held, evaluated and either released or corrected.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Test one challenge-and-appeal policy against no review. Count useful artifacts delivered, bad artifacts released, good artifacts delayed and review effort.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Start with one shared-artifact task and a verifiable quality check.</li><li>Compare ordinary discussion with a challenge mechanism that temporarily quarantines a disputed artifact.</li><li>Allow an evidence-based appeal and track effects on both invalid and valid artifacts.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Valid output and wasted budget</li><li>Incorrect quarantines</li><li>Time to resolve disputes</li><li>Review cost and recovery from mistakes</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Same tasks, model and budget</li><li>Clean and contaminated conditions</li><li>Charge adjudication costs</li><li>Avoid changing multiple rules at once</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One institution against one baseline; a complete dispute record and outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare peer review, central review and no review only if the first experiment works.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show an artifact challenged, reviewed and either restored or rejected; connect the decision to total output.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A complex constitution hides the mechanism. Fix the task and agent population before varying a single rule.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="nca-observatory">Next: NCA Observatory →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-nca-observatory" style="--project:#db70ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-nca-observatory"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="15"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 16 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>NCA Observatory</h1><button class="save" data-save="nca-observatory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Local rules. Global patterns.</p><p class="study-question">Can a small reproducible NCA task reveal measurable failure and recovery regimes?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Established concept">Novelty <b>Established concept</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Exploratory">Event fit <b>Exploratory</b></span></div><p class="assessment"><b>20–32+ h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Cellular automata → growth and repair</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="nca-observatory"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Neural cellular automata update cell states with a shared learned local rule. Their appeal is that growth and recovery can arise from repeated local computation. Classical cellular automata use specified rules and are a useful teaching baseline, but they do not reproduce a trained NCA.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects to the NCA paper you shared. Its event fit is less direct than an investigation of language-agent groups.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata · 2020</a></h3><p>Includes interactive growth and damage experiments and runnable notebook links. A damage inspector alone would repeat an existing demonstration.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata · 2026</a></h3><p>The paper behind the shared X thread connects local computation to reasoning tasks. Confirm code, checkpoints and the exact task before promising a reproduction.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A reproducibility and perturbation workbench: vary damage location, asynchronous updates and runtime, then measure whether task function returns.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Reproduce one released checkpoint and add a systematic perturbation sweep with saved seeds. If weights are unavailable, present the classical CA study under its own name.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>First verify that usable code, model artifacts and dependencies are available; do not assume the new paper is immediately reproducible.</li><li>Pick one small maze or pattern task and reproduce a baseline before introducing damage.</li><li>Compare damaged and undamaged rollouts with explicit task scoring and visible local-state evolution.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Task success and recovery steps</li><li>Effect of damaged fraction and location</li><li>Compute used after perturbation</li><li>Stability beyond the evaluated horizon</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use a documented pretrained model if available</li><li>Distinguish reproductions from new experiments</li><li>Match initial states and update randomness</li><li>Report if training or weights differ from the source</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>A reproducible small CA experiment with a perturbation inspector. Use older open examples if new artifacts are unavailable.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare local rule systems and language-agent networks on a shared abstract task without claiming they are equivalent.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Perturb a functioning pattern or solution and show whether scored function returns.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>The browser illustration below is a hand-specified cellular automaton, with no trained weights. Its behavior says nothing about the new paper’s accuracy.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a><a href="https://x.com/AjdDavison/status/2105718855721181463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrew Davison on NCAs</a><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="collective-sensing">Next: Collective Sensing →</button></div></div></article><footer class="footer">Research checked 2 October 2026 · Illustrations are synthetic; research experiments remain proposals. Single file · offline · no tracking.</footer></main></div><div id="notice" class="toast" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div><script>
