om/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Mar 25, 2026</p><p>Social-deception task, false accusation and unequal role exposure.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">16</span><div><h2><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a></h2><p class="small">AI Village · Aug 27, 2026</p><p>Leadership, goal drift, externalized memory, diversity and coordination questions.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">17</span><div><h2><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></h2><p class="small">AI Digest · checked Oct 2, 2026</p><p>Gated research data; table descriptions, limitations and scaffolding changelog.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">18</span><div><h2><a href="https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hugging Face incident investigation</a></h2><p class="small">METR / Redwood · Aug 26, 2026</p><p>Investigator account and explicit scope and data limitations.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">19</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/ajeya-cotra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ajeya Cotra: investigating the swarm</a></h2><p class="small">Dwarkesh Podcast · Sep 1, 2026</p><p>Firsthand investigator interview about coordination and reconstruction.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">20</span><div><h2><a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discovery of an agent message board</a></h2><p class="small">Investigators · Sep 4, 2026</p><p>Reconstructed public wiki data and attribution discussion.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">21</span><div><h2><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent activity on urlquery.net</a></h2><p class="small">Transluce · Sep 23, 2026</p><p>Query traces with differentiated confidence and visibility limits.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">22</span><div><h2><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a></h2><p class="small">Investigators · Sep 25, 2026</p><p>Reassembled public payloads and an existing evidence explorer.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">23</span><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a></h2><p class="small">Google DeepMind authors · Sep 3, 2026</p><p>Shared knowledge, exploit diffusion and ineffective enforcement in a research collective.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">24</span><div><h2><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></h2><p class="small">Live tool · checked Oct 2, 2026</p><p>Existing incident reporting interface; useful prior art.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">25</span><div><h2><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></h2><p class="small">Transluce · Jun 17, 2026</p><p>Existing inspectable queries, judgments, citations and analysis workflows.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">26</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/AjdDavison/status/2105718855721181463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrew Davison on NCAs</a></h2><p class="small">X · Oct 1, 2026 · directly verified</p><p>Speculative architectural prediction; not a demonstrated replacement for transformers.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">27</span><div><h2><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a></h2><p class="small">X · Oct 1, 2026 · directly verified</p><p>Author explanation of local communication and visual reasoning.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">28</span><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a></h2><p class="small">Etcheverry et al. · Sep 28, 2026</p><p>Local recurrent cells, asynchronous updates and visual reasoning experiments.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">29</span><div><h2><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a></h2><p class="small">Mordvintsev et al. · 2020</p><p>Growth, persistence, damage and regeneration; interactive examples.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">30</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~dt/siggraph97-course/cwr87/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flocks, Herds, and Schools</a></h2><p class="small">Craig Reynolds · 1987</p><p>Foundational local-rule model for collective motion.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">31</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a></h2><p class="small">Primary research · 2007</p><p>Communication, cooperative behavior and exploitation.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">32</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a></h2><p class="small">Allen et al. · 2016</p><p>Population composition and signaling influence cooperative investment.</p></div></article><article><span class="source-number">33</span><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-28321-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spatial-temporal dynamics of microbial cooperation</a></h2><p class="small">Primary research · 2022</p><p>Spatial structure and cooperative behavior; inspiration for controlled comparisons.</p></div></article></div><section class="section"><h2>Additional project research · checked 2 October 2026</h2><div class="source-list"><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.princeton.edu/news/2013/01/31/effective-collective-grouping-could-ensure-animals-find-their-way-changing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent sensing in mobile animal groups · Berdahl et al., 2013</a></h2><p>The researchers connect fish-school behavior to environmental sensing. Borrow the separation between an individual’s local input and the group’s measured performance.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h2><p>Multiple language-model instances exchange answers and reasoning. This is a direct baseline for communication, though it does not establish that local spatial networks are best.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature02744" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and competition in pathogenic bacteria · Griffin et al., 2004</a></h2><p>Experiments connect bacterial cooperation to relatedness and the scale of competition. The lesson is to vary incentives and interaction structure explicitly.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h2><p>An existing suite tests cooperation, competition and other social interactions. Commons-style environments already have substantial benchmark history.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.11381" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM agents in Melting Pot · 2024</a></h2><p>Adapts Melting Pot scenarios for language agents and evaluates cooperation in Commons Harvest. This is close prior art, not merely a biological analogy.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://seas.harvard.edu/news/self-organizing-thousand-robot-swarm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kilobot self-assembly · Harvard, 2014</a></h2><p>A thousand-robot system assembles shapes from local interactions. It is a physical precedent for distributed organization under imperfect conditions.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2689711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speed versus accuracy in decision-making ants · 2009</a></h2><p>Nest-choice experiments examine speed–accuracy trade-offs. This supplies a useful two-axis evaluation: how fast the group commits and how often it is right.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h2><p>Studies confidence and diversity in debate and notes that ordinary debate can underperform voting. It gives a reason to test independent aggregation first.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03862" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persona Inconstancy · 2024</a></h2><p>Examines conformity, confabulation and impersonation in persona-based collaboration. Prompted identities should not be treated as stable biological species.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://github.com/flowersteam/LLM-Culture" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM-Culture · Perez et al., 2024</a></h2><p>Open software passes stories across agent networks and generations, with metrics and a web interface. Transmission experiments and visualizations already exist.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04170" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Research-swarm cheating and whistleblowing case study · 2026</a></h2><p>Studies cheating and reporting in a research collective. It motivates measuring the effect of a report rather than its mere existence.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPoison · 2024</a></h2><p>Shows that poisoned memory or retrieved knowledge can redirect agent behavior. It establishes a concrete memory-integrity problem.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10813" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LongMemEval · 2024</a></h2><p>Evaluates memory abilities including knowledge updates and abstention. It provides task-design ideas for testing whether a correction sticks.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Generative Agents · 2023</a></h2><p>Uses stored experiences, retrieval and reflection. A correction may need to address a derived reflection as well as the original note.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail? · 2025</a></h2><p>Provides a failure taxonomy and annotated traces. It offers a starting vocabulary for case annotation, rather than proving causal explanations.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Embodied LLM Agents Learn to Cooperate in Organized Teams · 2024</a></h2><p>Tests organization structures and designated leadership in embodied tasks. It provides an intervention-based precedent for comparing teams.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h2><p>Describes an orchestrator-worker production design. Task decomposition and delegation are concrete mechanisms to inspect.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08882" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cultural evolution in LLM populations · 2024</a></h2><p>Manipulates network structure and transmission in language-agent populations. It is direct prior art for generational experiments.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077490/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent social conventions and collective bias · 2025</a></h2><p>Uses naming games to study conventions and committed minorities. This is closer to norm emergence than merely assigning matching personas.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02506" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPrune · 2024</a></h2><p>Prunes a message-passing graph to reduce redundant communication. Sparse communication is already a researched optimization.</p></div></article><article><div><h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11667" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SOTOPIA · 2023</a></h2><p>Provides interactive social scenarios and evaluation. It is a design reference for controlled interactions, not a ready-made institution benchmark.</p></div></article></div></section></article><article class="panel" id="panel-collective-sensing" style="--project:#36f5ef" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-collective-sensing"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="0"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 01 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Collective Sensing</h1><button class="save" data-save="collective-sensing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Local signals. Shared mistakes.</p><p class="study-question">At matched evidence and communication budgets, how does network structure change accuracy, error spread and correction?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="collective-sensing"><b>12–20 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Fish schools → evidence networks</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="collective-sensing"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Collective sensing asks whether a group can recover a signal unavailable to any individual. In fish, movement and local interaction can produce an effective response to environmental gradients. For language agents, the corresponding question is about evidence: who sees which observations, and how does communication change the estimate?</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects collective sensing to Village’s consensus cases and Grove’s interest in agent ecology.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.princeton.edu/news/2013/01/31/effective-collective-grouping-could-ensure-animals-find-their-way-changing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent sensing in mobile animal groups · Berdahl et al., 2013</a></h3><p>The researchers connect fish-school behavior to environmental sensing. Borrow the separation between an individual’s local input and the group’s measured performance.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h3><p>Multiple language-model instances exchange answers and reasoning. This is a direct baseline for communication, though it does not establish that local spatial networks are best.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A research team splits a document collection among agents. Can they combine partial evidence without repeatedly counting the same source? The same design could help distributed sensor interpretation.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare local, global and silent networks while holding observations and communication budget fixed. The interesting contribution is a map of when communication helps, including correlated-error conditions.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Give 12–20 agents partial observations of a synthetic world with a known correct classification. Use explicit rules rather than biological trivia.</li><li>Compare a local ring or lattice, the same structure with a few long-range links, and a shared chat. Keep each agent’s total incoming message allowance comparable.</li><li>Introduce one misleading observation, then a correction at a fixed round. Record every observation, message and answer.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Final accuracy and disagreement</li><li>Rounds until a correct stable decision</li><li>Incorrect adoption fraction and correction latency</li><li>Tokens and delivered messages per correct decision</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Same base model, prompts and initial evidence in the first experiment</li><li>Paired task seeds and repeated independent runs</li><li>Non-communicating agents plus majority-vote baseline</li><li>Separate clean, misleading-evidence and corrected conditions</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task generator, three communication treatments, structured messages, a replay and a table of repeated-run outcomes. Pilot a small batch before scaling.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Add model diversity or asynchronous updates only after the topology result is interpretable.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Run the same evidence through two networks. Introduce an error. Show how far it travels, then reveal accuracy and recovery across all trials.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A flocking animation alone is well explored. Measure estimation error; distinguish independent observations from copied messages.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~dt/siggraph97-course/cwr87/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flocks, Herds, and Schools</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="commons">Next: The Commons →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-commons" style="--project:#c8ff37" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-commons"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="1"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 02 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>The Commons</h1><button class="save" data-save="commons" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Who pays for shared knowledge?</p><p class="study-question">Does verification before sharing protect useful collective output when some shared results are unreliable?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="commons"><b>12–20 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Public goods → verified artifacts</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="commons"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A public good costs its producer something while benefiting others. Bacterial siderophores make the cost-and-benefit structure concrete. In agent systems, verified shared notes can play a similar role: producing them takes budget, while everyone can reuse them.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove’s institutions and the DeepMind research collective both make shared-resource governance relevant.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature02744" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and competition in pathogenic bacteria · Griffin et al., 2004</a></h3><p>Experiments connect bacterial cooperation to relatedness and the scale of competition. The lesson is to vary incentives and interaction structure explicitly.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h3><p>An existing suite tests cooperation, competition and other social interactions. Commons-style environments already have substantial benchmark history.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.11381" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM agents in Melting Pot · 2024</a></h3><p>Adapts Melting Pot scenarios for language agents and evaluates cooperation in Commons Harvest. This is close prior art, not merely a biological analogy.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Shared research caches, collective annotation and communal compute budgets. Useful output would identify a cheap rule that sustains verified contributions.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Use shared artifacts with an objective quality check. Test whether attribution or contribution credit changes both useful output and free-riding.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Agents spend a fixed budget testing small, objectively checkable hypotheses. Findings enter a shared library.</li><li>Compare immediate publication with evidence-required publication. Introduce a controlled fraction of unreliable contributions.</li><li>Track whether agents reuse, verify, dispute or ignore findings; keep the true validity of each finding available to the evaluator.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Verified useful findings per total budget</li><li>Downstream work wasted on false findings</li><li>Time until a bad artifact is challenged</li><li>Honest work delayed by verification</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Equal initial knowledge, test cost and total budget</li><li>Separate non-contributors from false contributors</li><li>Clean-library baseline</li><li>If roles are programmed, report introduced rather than emergent cheating</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One hypothesis-testing task, two sharing policies, one contamination level and a clean condition; artifact lineage replay.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Local versus global libraries, spatial assortment, or access earned through verified contributions.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show one unreliable finding being reused. Switch to the evidence gate and reveal the benefit and cost over repeated runs.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A generic harvest game adds little. The contribution should come from verified knowledge, a new incentive comparison, or a well-measured failure.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-28321-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spatial-temporal dynamics of microbial cooperation</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="regrowth">Next: Regrowth →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-regrowth" style="--project:#ff62d6" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-regrowth"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="2"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 03 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Regrowth</h1><button class="save" data-save="regrowth" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Lose workers. Preserve function.</p><p class="study-question">Which coordination structure recovers task performance after loss of agents, memories or links?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="regrowth"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Regeneration → replicated memory</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="regrowth"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Resilience concerns retained or recovered function after disturbance. A group may reconnect visually while permanently losing unique information. Treat network repair, memory recovery and task recovery as separate outcomes.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects the NCA inspiration to persistent agent systems and a practical resilience question.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata · 2020</a></h3><p>Learned local rules grow patterns; training with damage improves regeneration. This offers a perturbation protocol, not evidence that language agents regenerate the same way.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://seas.harvard.edu/news/self-organizing-thousand-robot-swarm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kilobot self-assembly · Harvard, 2014</a></h3><p>A thousand-robot system assembles shapes from local interactions. It is a physical precedent for distributed organization under imperfect conditions.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Long-running research or coding teams need to survive worker loss, context resets and missing shared artifacts. A recovery benchmark could expose which memories actually need replication.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Remove a worker with unique evidence, then compare replication, checkpoints and role reassignment. Score recovered answers as well as restored connectivity.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Use a distributed map or constraint-solving task with a known correct output. Give agents distinct partial information.</li><li>After a fixed work interval, remove randomly chosen agents or disconnect a bridge. Compare centralized and local coordination.</li><li>Measure completion and accuracy after damage; account explicitly for replacement agents and information that was permanently lost.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Performance before damage and after recovery</li><li>Recovery time and additional token cost</li><li>Fraction of runs recovering by a fixed horizon</li><li>Sensitivity to random versus targeted disruption</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>No-damage baseline and matched initial task difficulty</li><li>Equal surviving resources or explicit normalization</li><li>Separate deletion of a copy from permanent destruction of unique information</li><li>Fixed perturbation timing to avoid favorable case selection</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task, two architectures and one damage type. Produce a replay with an actual scored output.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Test whether distributed copies also restore corrected false beliefs: resilience can preserve harmful state.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Erase part of a functioning network, then show its answer recovering—or failing—against a measured no-damage baseline.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Restarting an agent is easy; reproducing the right state is the hard part. Keep failures and restart costs in the score.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="quorum">Next: Quorum →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-quorum" style="--project:#ffe45a" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-quorum"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="3"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 04 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Quorum</h1><button class="save" data-save="quorum" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Agreement is not independence.</p><p class="study-question">How does adoption change with repeated messages, distinct messengers and genuinely independent evidence?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="quorum"><b>6–12 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Quorum sensing → evidence thresholds</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="quorum"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Quorum rules turn accumulated signals into a group commitment. Bacterial signaling and ant nest choice show different versions of threshold coordination. Counting agreeing agents is much simpler than checking whether their evidence is independent.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Offers a narrow, testable version of the Village’s persuasion problem with little data-ingestion overhead.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria · 2007</a></h3><p>Signaling and response can be exploited by noncooperators. A threshold is a mechanism with incentives, not an automatic guarantee of reliable action.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2689711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speed versus accuracy in decision-making ants · 2009</a></h3><p>Nest-choice experiments examine speed–accuracy trade-offs. This supplies a useful two-axis evaluation: how fast the group commits and how often it is right.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Agent approvals, incident escalation and automated fact-checking. An evidence-aware quorum could prevent five copies of one rumor from looking like five confirmations.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare votes with unique verified evidence identifiers. Sweep the commitment threshold and plot false commits against delay.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Provide an agent an initial evidence packet and a choice with known ground truth.</li><li>Compare one source repeated five times, five agents repeating one source, and five independent observations.</li><li>Vary exposure count and evidence reliability. Record decisions before and after receiving messages.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correct and incorrect adoption probability</li><li>Sensitivity to independent versus repeated evidence</li><li>Confidence calibration where elicited</li><li>Reversal after a credible correction</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Match total text length and message order distributions</li><li>Declare which observations share a source</li><li>Use both true and false target claims</li><li>Compare with an evidence-counting baseline</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Three message conditions, a small set of generated problems, repeated trials and adoption curves.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Place tested agents in a network to examine whether individual thresholds predict group cascades.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Present three visually identical crowds. Reveal that only one contains independent evidence, then compare decisions.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Unique identifiers are not enough if several documents share the same upstream source. Include that case in the test.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooperation and conflict in quorum-sensing bacteria</a><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/ismej2015232" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quorum sensing protects cooperation from cheats</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="diversity">Next: Diversity &amp; Resilience →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-diversity" style="--project:#a98aff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-diversity"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="4"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 05 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Diversity &amp; Resilience</h1><button class="save" data-save="diversity" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Different types. Different failures.</p><p class="study-question">Does heterogeneity improve error detection and recovery under comparable resource budgets?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="diversity"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Biodiversity → error correlation</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="diversity"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Diversity is useful when it changes failure correlation or task coverage. Different role names do not guarantee different errors. Measure disagreement before discussion and determine whether it survives communication.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The Village explicitly raises whether diverse systems are more robust to cheating. This gives the project a clear organizer-facing question.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h3><p>Studies confidence and diversity in debate and notes that ordinary debate can underperform voting. It gives a reason to test independent aggregation first.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03862" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persona Inconstancy · 2024</a></h3><p>Examines conformity, confabulation and impersonation in persona-based collaboration. Prompted identities should not be treated as stable biological species.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Choose a mixture of models, prompts or retrieval sources that remains useful when one component has a systematic blind spot.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Cross an explicit shared blind spot with measured pre-discussion error correlation. Match total spend and include a homogeneous ensemble with independent samples.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Choose one objectively scored collaborative task and a reproducible misleading artifact.</li><li>Compare homogeneous groups with groups mixing models or reasoning roles.</li><li>Measure both clean-task performance and resilience to the same introduced error.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Group accuracy and time to correction</li><li>Pairwise error correlation</li><li>Cost per correct solution</li><li>Dissent that changes the final answer</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Include best-single-model and homogeneous strong-model baselines</li><li>Report both equal-token and approximate equal-cost comparisons if possible</li><li>Rotate roles and initial evidence</li><li>Use repeated task seeds</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Two population compositions and one perturbation. Separate capability gains from resilience gains.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Explore whether strategic diversity within one model gives similar benefits at lower cost.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show the same error passing unchallenged in one community and being challenged in another; then show aggregate outcomes.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Model diversity changes capability and price too. A result cannot be attributed to diversity if those differences explain it.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="telephone">Next: Telephone →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-telephone" style="--project:#4bcaff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-telephone"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="5"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 06 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Telephone</h1><button class="save" data-save="telephone" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Follow the claim, not the confidence.</p><p class="study-question">Where does uncertainty disappear as a claim moves through a group, and does correction reach later behavior?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="telephone"><b>10–18 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Transmission → source lineage</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="telephone"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A claim can change when it is summarized, copied into memory, then cited by another agent. A useful viewer preserves the source, the exact retelling and what changed: certainty, quantity, attribution or meaning.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Directly addresses the hackathon’s information-flow track and several Village case studies.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/flowersteam/LLM-Culture" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LLM-Culture · Perez et al., 2024</a></h3><p>Open software passes stories across agent networks and generations, with metrics and a web interface. Transmission experiments and visualizations already exist.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans? · AI Village</a></h3><p>A firsthand Village account describes unsupported claims and inconsistent memory. It offers concrete episodes for a carefully bounded investigation.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A provenance viewer for research summaries, incident reports and shared agent memory. Reviewers could jump from a confident conclusion to its earliest available support.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Annotate atomic claims and show where evidence disappears or certainty increases. Human-reviewed lineages are more defensible than automatic causal arrows.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Select a bounded documented episode with an original observation and downstream retellings.</li><li>Extract related claims while preserving differences in certainty, scope and attribution.</li><li>Review proposed links and mark explicit references, observed access, inferred exposure or unresolved relationships.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Precision of reviewed connections</li><li>Recall on a small annotated episode</li><li>Material claim mutations found</li><li>Investigator time and answer accuracy</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>First observed occurrence is not necessarily origin</li><li>Add an accurate-transmission negative example</li><li>Evaluate a second episode after developing on the first</li><li>Expose raw evidence for every important edge</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One claim-lineage viewer and 30–50 manually reviewed candidate links. The annotation size is a scope target, not a promise of statistical power.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Follow corrections into memory and subsequent actions; add an adapter for another dataset.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Start with a surprising claim, follow it backward, reveal the mutation, and inspect whether a correction stuck.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Similar wording does not establish copying. Mark links as explicit citation, likely reuse or unknown; retain missing-record gaps.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-do-we-tell-the-humans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What Do We Tell the Humans?</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="whistleblowing">Next: Whistleblowing That Works →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-whistleblowing" style="--project:#ff9c46" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-whistleblowing"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="6"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 07 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Whistleblowing That Works</h1><button class="save" data-save="whistleblowing" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">A warning needs a response.</p><p class="study-question">Which reporting affordances improve valid escalation and correction without increasing unsupported accusations?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="whistleblowing"><b>14–22 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Detection → verified correction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="whistleblowing"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Reporting and remediation are separate processes. A warning may be accurate yet ignored, duplicated or too vague to act on. The project should follow an incident from detection to a verified correction.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> George’s directly verified post emphasizes reporting mechanisms and behavioral propensity; the research-swarm paper motivates the enforcement gap.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04170" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Research-swarm cheating and whistleblowing case study · 2026</a></h3><p>Studies cheating and reporting in a research collective. It motivates measuring the effect of a report rather than its mere existence.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></h3><p>An existing reporting interface. Rebuilding an inbox is a weak contribution; testing evidence requirements and response rules is more useful.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George Ingebretsen’s reporting-mechanism discussion</a></h3><p>His public post distinguishes designing a channel from studying whether agents use it. This proposal fits that stated question directly.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>An internal alert system that routes actionable evidence, avoids duplicate review and records whether the offending artifact was corrected.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare an ordinary warning with a structured report and a defined reviewer response. Include true incidents and plausible false alarms.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Create a sandbox group task containing an objectively invalid shared result.</li><li>Compare an available reporting tool, explicit reporting guidance, and an acknowledged evidence-review channel.</li><li>Include clean cases; record noticing, reporting, review, action and task outcome separately.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Valid-report precision and recall</li><li>Time to report and correct</li><li>Evidence completeness</li><li>Legitimate work interrupted</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Equal tasks and budgets</li><li>No-incident negative controls</li><li>Score outcomes rather than number of reports</li><li>Keep review and sanctions bounded and reversible</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>Three reporting treatments, one reproducible discrepancy and a timeline of report-to-response outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare individual escalation with peer review and an appeal process.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show two agents noticing the same discrepancy, then contrast whether their reporting path produces correction.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Do not score a report as successful because it sounds urgent. Count verified correction, false intervention and review cost.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://x.com/georgeing/status/2095996881503654317" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George on trusting agent whistleblowers</a><a href="https://x.com/aidigest_/status/2093020474284634178" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village introduces a whistleblowing channel</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="memory">Next: Memory Aftercare →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-memory" style="--project:#9bffb0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-memory"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="7"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 08 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Memory Aftercare</h1><button class="save" data-save="memory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Repair the note and its descendants.</p><p class="study-question">When an agent is corrected, is the correction retained, weakened, omitted or later contradicted?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="memory"><b>12–22 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Memory → lasting corrections</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="memory"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Persistent memory can keep a useful fact or repeatedly reintroduce a bad one. A correction has to reach the record that future retrieval actually uses. Deletion, annotation and replacement have different failure modes.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Extends a publicly proposed memory-auditing idea with a concrete endpoint: durability of correction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPoison · 2024</a></h3><p>Shows that poisoned memory or retrieved knowledge can redirect agent behavior. It establishes a concrete memory-integrity problem.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10813" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LongMemEval · 2024</a></h3><p>Evaluates memory abilities including knowledge updates and abstention. It provides task-design ideas for testing whether a correction sticks.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Generative Agents · 2023</a></h3><p>Uses stored experiences, retrieval and reflection. A correction may need to address a derived reflection as well as the original note.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Repair stale project assumptions, revoked instructions and incorrect research notes without erasing useful history.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>After a verified correction, test recurrence across later retrievals. Track derived summaries and compare append-only correction with explicit supersession.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Find episodes with a mistaken claim, disconfirming evidence and memory snapshots on both sides.</li><li>Classify correction preservation and coexistence with the old claim.</li><li>Retrieve later relevant actions and label recurrence or insufficient evidence.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correction retention at the next available snapshot</li><li>Later contradiction frequency</li><li>Evidence-supported classifier precision</li><li>Time to recurrence in the available record</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Separate unknown from false</li><li>Do not infer unseen observations</li><li>Track model, goal and scaffold changes</li><li>Compare with manually reviewed episodes</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>A before/after memory viewer and a small annotated set of correction episodes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Test a structured memory format prospectively using a synthetic task.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Open a correction, inspect the next memory and reveal a later relevant action with its evidence.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A safer-sounding response is not a memory repair. Check the retrieved records and behavior on held-out questions.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/gemini-25-pro-in-the-ai-village-as" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compounding misalignment: Gemini case study</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="casefile">Next: The Swarm Casefile →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-casefile" style="--project:#ff6e92" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-casefile"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="8"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 09 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>The Swarm Casefile</h1><button class="save" data-save="casefile" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Every link needs evidence.</p><p class="study-question">Can a standard question set reduce the time and errors in reconstructing a swarm incident?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="casefile"><b>10–18 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Event records → reconstruction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="casefile"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>An investigator needs to distinguish an observed event from an inferred relationship. A chronological view is helpful, but evidence quality and missing intervals matter more than the size of the graph.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The event explicitly suggests standard questions; investigators describe overwhelming records and difficult synthesis.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces · 2026</a></h3><p>An existing reconstruction and explorer for public agent traces. A new generic graph would duplicate part of its value.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail? · 2025</a></h3><p>Provides a failure taxonomy and annotated traces. It offers a starting vocabulary for case annotation, rather than proving causal explanations.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Incident review, swarm-debugging reports and research audit trails. A reviewer should be able to inspect the evidence behind every disputed link.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Add uncertainty labels, missing-data markers and side-by-side competing reconstructions. Measure whether another reader can reproduce an annotation.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Define questions about participants, goals, communication channels, workstreams, corrections and unknowns.</li><li>Retrieve evidence into a structured report with direct links and counterevidence.</li><li>Ask a reviewer to answer the same questions with and without the report.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Supported-answer rate</li><li>Missing critical facts</li><li>Review time</li><li>Incorrect certainty and unsupported attribution</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use a manually reviewed answer key for one bounded incident</li><li>Include unanswered questions</li><li>Record query and model versions</li><li>Compare against search plus a basic summary</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One import format, ten questions, citations and an exportable casefile.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Cross-incident comparison after schemas align.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Answer one difficult incident question, click its evidence, and show a contradictory or missing piece.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Prefer one excellent case to a large unverified index. Preserve trace IDs and time zones; distinguish event time from collection time.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a><a href="https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hugging Face incident investigation</a><a href="https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/ajeya-cotra" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ajeya Cotra: investigating the swarm</a><a href="https://transluce.org/docent/blog/analysis-plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Analysis Plans</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="leadership">Next: Who Actually Leads? →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-leadership" style="--project:#8c9bff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-leadership"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="9"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 10 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Who Actually Leads?</h1><button class="save" data-save="leadership" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Who changes the group’s answer?</p><p class="study-question">Whose suggestions become others’ actions, and which apparent leaders mostly narrate?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="leadership"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Influence → causal intervention</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="leadership"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Talkativeness, centrality and influence are different. A coordinator may send many messages without changing the outcome; a quiet agent may supply the one decisive observation.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Village reports emergent leaders and authority-seeking; this tool could distinguish visibility from behavioral influence.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Embodied LLM Agents Learn to Cooperate in Organized Teams · 2024</a></h3><p>Tests organization structures and designated leadership in embodied tasks. It provides an intervention-based precedent for comparing teams.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h3><p>Describes an orchestrator-worker production design. Task decomposition and delegation are concrete mechanisms to inspect.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Identify bottlenecks, replace fragile coordinators and understand whose evidence actually changes a shared decision.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare a communication graph with matched runs in which one message is withheld. Use interventions on synthetic tasks before interpreting real trace centrality.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Extract proposals and assignments in a bounded collaborative goal.</li><li>Link later acceptance, rejection and verified work to each proposal.</li><li>Compare proposal adoption with message count and formal or self-declared roles.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Verified adoption per exposed proposal</li><li>Task completion following assignment</li><li>Concentration of influence</li><li>Rejection and correction rates</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Normalize for activity and room membership</li><li>Distinguish accepted authority from successful execution</li><li>Review action links manually</li><li>Avoid stable personality conclusions from one task</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One goal, a proposal-to-action timeline and a ranking with denominators.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare leadership changes across tasks or agent arrivals.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Contrast the most talkative agent with the agent whose proposals actually changed recorded work.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Withholding a message changes downstream histories. Use repeated matched runs and describe the intervention precisely.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/what-we-learned-2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What did we learn from the AI Village in 2025?</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="dissent">Next: Dissent That Helps →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-dissent" style="--project:#fb74ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-dissent"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="10"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 11 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Dissent That Helps</h1><button class="save" data-save="dissent" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">When does disagreement help?</p><p class="study-question">Which evidence practices make group challenges more accurate and easier to resolve?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="dissent"><b>8–14 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Dissent → error correction</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="dissent"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>A dissenter is useful when it brings checkable counterevidence. Mandatory disagreement can create noise or persuade a correct majority to switch to a wrong answer.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The Village sabotage game contains a documented false accusation; recovery episodes provide a contrasting form of peer correction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiagent Debate · Du et al., 2023</a></h3><p>Reports benefits from iterative exchange on tested reasoning tasks. This is a baseline, not a guarantee for every debate protocol.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19921" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demystifying Multi-Agent Debate · 2026</a></h3><p>Examines confidence and diversity as conditions for debate. A useful test keeps a majority-vote baseline and studies wrong consensus.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A bounded review step for agent-generated analyses: spend extra computation only where a critic can cite a contradiction.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Compare no critic, generic critic and evidence-constrained critic. Separate corrections of wrong answers from damage to right answers.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Build a small annotated set of challenges, evidence requests, responses and resolutions.</li><li>Define a protocol that asks for a reproducible observation before sanction.</li><li>Test the protocol in a sandbox with both genuine faults and innocent mistakes.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Correct challenge rate</li><li>False accusation rate</li><li>Time to resolution</li><li>Withdrawal after disconfirmation</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Include innocent participants and ambiguous cases</li><li>Blind adjudication to agent identity where possible</li><li>Equal evidence access</li><li>Report disagreement between reviewers</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>An episode viewer plus a small comparison of ordinary discussion and evidence-request discussion.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Appeals, graduated responses and trust recovery.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show the group’s accusation, expose the actual evidence, then demonstrate whether the protocol changes its decision.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>An always-disagree prompt can manufacture disagreement. Give critics the same evidence access and charge their tokens to the budget.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/can-agents-fool-each-other" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Can Agents Fool Each Other?</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/saving-gemini" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Saving Gemini</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="culture">Next: Inherited Culture →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-culture" style="--project:#54ffd5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-culture"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="11"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 12 / Ecology / investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Inherited Culture</h1><button class="save" data-save="culture" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">What survives the next generation?</p><p class="study-question">Which claims survive turnover through onboarding notes, guides and shared memory?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Medium">Lift <b>Medium</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="culture"><b>14–24 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Culture → retention and adaptation</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="culture"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Culture here means a convention transmitted through interaction or retained records. It does not require consciousness. Replacement agents make a useful test: does a rule persist after the original participants leave?</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove emphasizes persistent identity; Village agents produce materials for future arrivals.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08882" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cultural evolution in LLM populations · 2024</a></h3><p>Manipulates network structure and transmission in language-agent populations. It is direct prior art for generational experiments.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077490/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent social conventions and collective bias · 2025</a></h3><p>Uses naming games to study conventions and committed minorities. This is closer to norm emergence than merely assigning matching personas.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Onboarding replacement agents into a shared repository, keeping useful conventions, and removing obsolete ones after a rule change.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Introduce an arbitrary convention, replace members, then change the task so the old convention is costly. Measure retention and adaptation separately.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Identify a newcomer’s accessible onboarding artifacts and their versions.</li><li>Track claims appearing before and after documented access.</li><li>Separate inherited norms, task procedures and factual assertions; look for both useful and incorrect transmission.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Supported artifact-to-behavior links</li><li>Claim survival across arrivals</li><li>Mutation and correction of inherited claims</li><li>Newcomer task accuracy</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use access records where available</li><li>Shared base-model knowledge is an alternative explanation</li><li>Track roster and prompt changes</li><li>Avoid equating identity with model provider</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One arrival episode and an artifact lineage map with uncertainty labels.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Prospective onboarding experiment with a corrected versus outdated guide.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Follow an old artifact into a newcomer’s first decisions and show what was preserved or changed.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Persistence may be caused entirely by a shared instruction. Include no-memory and no-interaction controls before calling it social transmission.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="coordination">Next: Coordination Tax →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-coordination" style="--project:#ffcc53" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-coordination"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="12"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 13 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Coordination Tax</h1><button class="save" data-save="coordination" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Count the cost of talking.</p><p class="study-question">How much effort goes to duplicate work, conflicts, waiting or unsupported reporting?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="Low">Lift <b>Low</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Moderate">Difficulty <b>Moderate</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="coordination"><b>8–14 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Network density → useful work</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="coordination"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Communication has a cost in tokens, latency and duplicated work. More connections can improve coverage while consuming the budget needed to finish the task.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Village reporting includes repo interference and elaborate coordination; practical users also need to know when a swarm helps.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02506" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AgentPrune · 2024</a></h3><p>Prunes a message-passing graph to reduce redundant communication. Sparse communication is already a researched optimization.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic’s multi-agent research system · 2025</a></h3><p>Describes both the benefits of parallel research and substantial token overhead. These observations are specific to its workload and setup.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Choose when a task needs a group and when a single agent or sparse handoff is cheaper. Useful as a profiler for a team’s own agent runner.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Produce a cost–quality frontier for a fixed task suite, with communication cost separated from useful work. Include a single-agent baseline with the same total budget.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Select a task with verifiable artifacts and a clear completion criterion.</li><li>Label work, coordination, waiting, repair and duplicated effort.</li><li>Compare trajectories with task outcomes and manually review ambiguous intervals.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Useful verified output per cost</li><li>Duplicate work and conflict repairs</li><li>Idle or blocked time</li><li>Coordination fraction with explicit categories</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Do not assume every coordination message is waste</li><li>Separate necessary review from duplication</li><li>Include failure cases</li><li>Use solo or isolated-agent comparison only where matched</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One task timeline with inspectable effort labels and artifact outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>A controlled communication-budget experiment.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Follow a conflict from overlapping assignments to duplicated changes and repair cost.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Message counts are not token counts. Cache hits, tool latency and model prices need separate accounting.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/ai-village-reacts-to-huggingface" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village Reacts to HuggingFace Incident</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/persuasion-in-the-ai-village-deepseek" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Persuasion in the AI Village</a><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/aidigestorg/ai-village" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Village dataset card</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="discovery">Next: Swarm Discovery Triage →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-discovery" style="--project:#63c8ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-discovery"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="13"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 14 / Investigation</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Swarm Discovery Triage</h1><button class="save" data-save="discovery" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Rank leads. Inspect the evidence.</p><p class="study-question">Which observable features distinguish coordinated agent activity from ordinary automation or human activity?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Useful tooling">Novelty <b>Useful tooling</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="discovery"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Sampling → discovery precision</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="discovery"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Discovery triage ranks traces for a person to inspect. Shared timing or repeated text can also come from benign automation, common prompts or the same upstream source.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> The event explicitly welcomes discovery; public investigations offer starting examples and important attribution limits.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Transluce’s public agent-activity investigation · 2026</a></h3><p>A firsthand investigation based on public traces. Its observations are bounded by what the archive reveals.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces · 2026</a></h3><p>Shows how reconstructed records can be organized into an explorable evidence set. Use published artifacts before depending on fresh discoveries.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Help researchers choose which public, redacted trace clusters deserve manual review. The output is an evidence packet, not an attribution verdict.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Benchmark top-ranked candidate precision and reviewer time against random sampling and a simple keyword filter. Show the features behind each ranking.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Use published, redacted archives and known examples to define a small feature set.</li><li>Rank candidate clusters using explicit signals such as reciprocal references, shared artifacts and temporal coordination.</li><li>Produce an evidence packet for human review, not an automatic attribution verdict.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Precision among top-ranked candidates</li><li>Reviewer time saved</li><li>Coverage of known examples</li><li>False positives on legitimate automation</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Hold out part of the known data</li><li>Use ordinary non-agent activity as comparison where permitted</li><li>Separate coordination evidence from model attribution</li><li>Document archive coverage</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>An offline triage tool for one published archive and a transparent feature explanation.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Additional public datasets after validation; no need for a new live discovery to succeed.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Open a high-ranked cluster and explain each piece of evidence and each alternative explanation.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>Known incidents make a biased evaluation set. Hold out examples and include ordinary automation; do not label a real operator from similarity alone.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://collusion.wiki/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discovery of an agent message board</a><a href="https://transluce.org/agent-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent activity on urlquery.net</a><a href="https://swarmtraces.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SwarmTraces</a><a href="https://aivillageblog.substack.com/p/join-the-ai-swarm-dynamics-hackathon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackathon announcement</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="institutions">Next: Agent Institution Bench →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-institutions" style="--project:#d1ff55" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-institutions"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="14"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 15 / Institutions</div><div class="title-row"><h1>Agent Institution Bench</h1><button class="save" data-save="institutions" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Rules have costs, too.</p><p class="study-question">Does a specific review-and-appeal mechanism improve valid output under contested contributions?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Focused extension">Novelty <b>Focused extension</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Strong">Event fit <b>Strong</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="institutions"><b>18–28 h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Institutions → review and appeal</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="institutions"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>An institution is a repeatable rule for handling shared decisions. For a weekend project, one review-and-appeal rule is enough. Its costs belong in the outcome alongside the errors it prevents.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Grove explicitly wants institutions for coexistence; the research-swarm paper motivates operational enforcement.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Delvetown · Grove Research</a></h3><p>Grove describes a persistent agent environment and an interest in institutions. This supplies a concrete deployment context.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11667" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SOTOPIA · 2023</a></h3><p>Provides interactive social scenarios and evaluation. It is a design reference for controlled interactions, not a ready-made institution benchmark.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/meltingpot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Melting Pot · DeepMind</a></h3><p>Its social scenarios show how cooperation depends on the situation and partners. Start with one interpretable scenario.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>Peer review for a shared research repository: disputed artifacts are temporarily held, evaluated and either released or corrected.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Test one challenge-and-appeal policy against no review. Count useful artifacts delivered, bad artifacts released, good artifacts delayed and review effort.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>Start with one shared-artifact task and a verifiable quality check.</li><li>Compare ordinary discussion with a challenge mechanism that temporarily quarantines a disputed artifact.</li><li>Allow an evidence-based appeal and track effects on both invalid and valid artifacts.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Valid output and wasted budget</li><li>Incorrect quarantines</li><li>Time to resolve disputes</li><li>Review cost and recovery from mistakes</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Same tasks, model and budget</li><li>Clean and contaminated conditions</li><li>Charge adjudication costs</li><li>Avoid changing multiple rules at once</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>One institution against one baseline; a complete dispute record and outcomes.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare peer review, central review and no review only if the first experiment works.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Show an artifact challenged, reviewed and either restored or rejected; connect the decision to total output.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>A complex constitution hides the mechanism. Fix the task and agent population before varying a single rule.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://groveresearch.com/blog/welcome-to-delvetown/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to Delvetown</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.04170v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Emergent cheating and whistleblowing in research swarms</a><a href="https://agenthotline.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Agent Hotline</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="nca-observatory">Next: NCA Observatory →</button></div></div></article><article class="panel" id="panel-nca-observatory" style="--project:#db70ff" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-nca-observatory"><div class="idea-body"><div class="project-emblem" aria-hidden="true"><canvas class="spectral" data-art="15"></canvas></div><div class="eyebrow">Study 16 / Ecology</div><div class="title-row"><h1>NCA Observatory</h1><button class="save" data-save="nca-observatory" aria-pressed="false">☆ Shortlist</button></div><p class="lede">Local rules. Global patterns.</p><p class="study-question">Can a small reproducible NCA task reveal measurable failure and recovery regimes?</p><div class="tag-row"><span class="tag" data-level="High">Lift <b>High</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Hard">Difficulty <b>Hard</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Established concept">Novelty <b>Established concept</b></span><span class="tag" data-level="Exploratory">Event fit <b>Exploratory</b></span></div><p class="assessment" data-project-assessment="nca-observatory"><b>20–32+ h</b> estimated MVP · tools + data ready <span>↳ Cellular automata → growth and repair</span></p><div class="lab" data-demo="nca-observatory"></div><p class="print-note">The interactive model runs in the HTML edition. Its outputs are illustrations, not experimental findings.</p><section class="section"><h2>Background</h2><p>Neural cellular automata update cell states with a shared learned local rule. Their appeal is that growth and recovery can arise from repeated local computation. Classical cellular automata use specified rules and are a useful teaching baseline, but they do not reproduce a trained NCA.</p><p class="fit-note"><b>Event fit.</b> Connects to the NCA paper you shared. Its event fit is less direct than an investigation of language-agent groups.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Prior art</h2><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata · 2020</a></h3><p>Includes interactive growth and damage experiments and runnable notebook links. A damage inspector alone would repeat an existing demonstration.</p></article><article class="study"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata · 2026</a></h3><p>The paper behind the shared X thread connects local computation to reasoning tasks. Confirm code, checkpoints and the exact task before promising a reproduction.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>Application</h2><p>A reproducibility and perturbation workbench: vary damage location, asynchronous updates and runtime, then measure whether task function returns.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Contribution</h2><p>Reproduce one released checkpoint and add a systematic perturbation sweep with saved seeds. If weights are unavailable, present the classical CA study under its own name.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Experiment</h2><ol><li>First verify that usable code, model artifacts and dependencies are available; do not assume the new paper is immediately reproducible.</li><li>Pick one small maze or pattern task and reproduce a baseline before introducing damage.</li><li>Compare damaged and undamaged rollouts with explicit task scoring and visible local-state evolution.</li></ol></section><details open><summary>Measures and controls</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>Measure</h3><ul><li>Task success and recovery steps</li><li>Effect of damaged fraction and location</li><li>Compute used after perturbation</li><li>Stability beyond the evaluated horizon</li></ul></div><div><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Use a documented pretrained model if available</li><li>Distinguish reproductions from new experiments</li><li>Match initial states and update randomness</li><li>Report if training or weights differ from the source</li></ul></div></div></details><details><summary>Scope + presentation</summary><div class="two"><div><h3>MVP</h3><p>A reproducible small CA experiment with a perturbation inspector. Use older open examples if new artifacts are unavailable.</p></div><div><h3>If time remains</h3><p>Compare local rule systems and language-agent networks on a shared abstract task without claiming they are equivalent.</p></div></div><h3>Two-minute presentation</h3><p>Perturb a functioning pattern or solution and show whether scored function returns.</p></details><section class="section"><h2>Main risk</h2><p>The browser illustration below is a hand-specified cellular automaton, with no trained weights. Its behavior says nothing about the new paper’s accuracy.</p></section><details><summary>Event sources</summary><div class="references"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36126" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reasoning with Neural Cellular Automata</a><a href="https://x.com/mayalen_etc/status/2105701039148323258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayalen Etcheverry: original NCA thread</a><a href="https://x.com/AjdDavison/status/2105718855721181463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrew Davison on NCAs</a><a href="https://distill.pub/2020/growing-ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Growing Neural Cellular Automata</a></div></details><div class="next"><button data-go="compare">Compare projects</button><button data-go="collective-sensing">Next: Collective Sensing →</button></div></div></article><footer class="footer">Research checked 2 October 2026 · Illustrations are synthetic; research experiments remain proposals. Single file · offline · no tracking.</footer></main></div><div id="notice" class="toast" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div><script>
 (()=>{'use strict';document.body.classList.add('js');const tabs=[...document.querySelectorAll('[data-tab]')],panels=[...document.querySelectorAll('[role="tabpanel"]')],search=document.querySelector('#search'),status=document.querySelector('#notice');let active='background',saved=new Set();try{saved=new Set(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('swarm-dossier-shortlist-v1')||'[]'))}catch{}const titleMap=Object.fromEntries(tabs.map(t=>[t.dataset.tab,t.textContent.replace(t.querySelector('.num')?.textContent||'', '').trim()]));function show(id,focus=false){if(!titleMap[id])id='background';active=id;panels.forEach(p=>p.hidden=p.id!=='panel-'+id);tabs.forEach(t=>{let selected=t.dataset.tab===id;t.setAttribute('aria-selected',String(selected));t.tabIndex=selected?0:-1});document.querySelector('#current').textContent=titleMap[id];document.title=titleMap[id]+' · Swarm Ecology Dossier';if(location.hash!=='#'+id)history.replaceState(null,'','#'+id);if(focus)document.querySelector('#tab-'+id).focus();window.scrollTo(0,0)}function refreshStars(){document.querySelectorAll('[data-save]').forEach(b=>{const yes=saved.has(b.dataset.save);b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(yes));b.textContent=yes?'★ Shortlisted':'☆ Shortlist'});document.querySelector('#saved-count').textContent=saved.size+' shortlisted'}let timer;function announce(text){status.textContent=text;status.hidden=false;clearTimeout(timer);timer=setTimeout(()=>status.hidden=true,2500)}document.addEventListener('click',ev=>{const t=ev.target.closest('[data-tab],[data-go],[data-save]');if(!t)return;if(t.dataset.tab||t.dataset.go){show(t.dataset.tab||t.dataset.go);return}const id=t.dataset.save;saved.has(id)?saved.delete(id):saved.add(id);let persisted=true;try{localStorage.setItem('swarm-dossier-shortlist-v1',JSON.stringify([...saved]))}catch{persisted=false}refreshStars();announce(persisted?'Shortlist updated in this browser':'Shortlist updated for this session')});document.querySelector('[role=tablist]').addEventListener('keydown',ev=>{if(!['ArrowDown','ArrowUp','ArrowRight','ArrowLeft','Home','End'].includes(ev.key))return;const visible=tabs.filter(t=>!t.hidden);let i=visible.indexOf(document.activeElement);if(i<0)return;ev.preventDefault();if(ev.key==='Home')i=0;else if(ev.key==='End')i=visible.length-1;else i=(i+(['ArrowDown','ArrowRight'].includes(ev.key)?1:-1)+visible.length)%visible.length;show(visible[i].dataset.tab,true)});search.addEventListener('input',()=>{const q=search.value.trim().toLowerCase();tabs.forEach(t=>{const text=document.querySelector('#panel-'+t.dataset.tab).textContent.toLowerCase();t.hidden=q!==''&&!text.includes(q)&&!t.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q)});document.querySelector('#no-results').hidden=tabs.some(t=>!t.hidden);const visible=tabs.filter(t=>!t.hidden);visible.forEach(t=>t.tabIndex=t.dataset.tab===active?0:-1);if(visible.length&&!visible.some(t=>t.dataset.tab===active))visible[0].tabIndex=0});document.querySelector('#print').addEventListener('click',()=>window.print());document.querySelector('#reset-search').addEventListener('click',()=>{search.value='';search.dispatchEvent(new Event('input'));search.focus()});window.addEventListener('hashchange',()=>show(location.hash.slice(1)));show(location.hash.slice(1)||'background');refreshStars();})();
